                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:27:37.782646029Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Found by ronin-clean-tree-fixture, invisible before it because no other workload has a .ninja_log. core::str::converts::from_utf8 costs 772,813 instructions, 2.78% of the clean-tree no-op, across 8,000 calls from log::BuildLog::open — the same idiom removed from the scanner under ronin-byte-keyed-names, still alive in the build-log reader. The four sites are src/log.rs:142, 146, 150 and 156: each takes a field from nextfield, validates it as UTF-8, and then parses an integer from it — start_time and end_time as i32-ish values, mtime, and the command hash through u64::from_str_radix(.., 16). All four fields are ASCII digits or hex by construction of the log format, so the validation is redundant exactly as it was in scan::name, and the fix is to parse the integer directly from the bytes rather than to go through str. The wider log path is worth the same visit while it is open: read_line (src/log.rs:168) is 3.60% and copies every line out of a BufRead into a reusable Vec via extend_from_slice, nextfield is 2.71%, BuildLog::open itself 1.52% and hydrate_runtime 0.60%, so the subsystem is around 11% of this workload and has never been measured or optimised at all. The per-line copy in read_line becomes unnecessary if the log is mapped rather than streamed, which is why this composes with ronin-mmap-inputs — sequence them together if both are taken, and attribute the measurement carefully if they land in one change. Allocation traffic on this workload is 9.1 requests per build statement against wide-noop's 2.1, and the log reader is the obvious suspect, so re-record the allocation baseline after."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:33:20.980764327Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Proposed approach: two small byte parsers, and scope the line reader out of this node. Replace the four str round trips with parse_decimal(&[u8]) -> Option<i64> and parse_hex(&[u8]) -> Option<u64>, each folding over the bytes with checked_mul and checked_add and returning None on any non-digit or on overflow. Both are about ten lines. The behavioural requirement is that a malformed field still yields None so the line is skipped and recovery proceeds exactly as now, which means matching what str::parse accepts at the edges: an optional leading '-' or '+' on the decimal fields, and rejecting an empty field. Ninja writes mtimes that can legitimately be negative, so the sign handling is not hypothetical. Test the parsers directly against the str implementations they replace over a table of valid, empty, signed, overflowing and non-numeric inputs, rather than only through the log reader, because the recovery path is what makes a wrong answer here silent. Deliberately out of scope: read_line's per-line copy, at 3.60%. Removing it means reading the whole log once and slicing it at newlines, taking min(len, MAX_LOG_LINE) per line to preserve the existing truncation, and that belongs with ronin-mmap-inputs where the buffer question is being decided. Doing it here would tangle the two measurements. Expect this node alone to be worth around the 2.78% from_utf8 costs, less whatever the byte parsers themselves cost, so a fraction of that — measure on clean-tree-noop, which is the only fixture that reads a log, and re-record the allocation baseline since this workload sits at 9.1 requests per build statement and the log reader is the suspect."
                            }

