                    {
                        id ronin-node-locality-at-scale
                        title "Find why per-edge cost grows faster than samurai's"
                        deps (ronin-large-manifest-fixture)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:28:24.724439063Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Ronin's per-edge cost grows with graph size faster than samurai's. Measured on parse-and-evaluate: ronin goes 1.95, 2.44, 2.67, 2.96 and 3.15 microseconds per edge at 4,000, 25,000, 50,000, 100,000 and 200,000 statements, a rise of 62%, while samurai goes 2.35, 2.75, 2.83, 2.99 and 2.97, a rise of 26% that is essentially flat past 50,000. Both tools grow, which is expected once working sets exceed cache, but ronin grows more than twice as fast, and that is what turns a 17% lead at fixture size into a 6% deficit at 200,000. Instruction counts do not explain it — the shape is characteristic of locality — so this needs measuring as a memory problem rather than an instruction-count one. Proposed approach: with perf now available, start with cache-references, cache-misses, dTLB-load-misses and instructions-per-cycle across the size sweep for both tools, and see whether ronin's miss rate diverges where the per-edge curves do. If it does, the first suspect is node path storage. ronin-path-interning-arena is marked Done but its own description set an endgame of a single append-only byte arena with nodes holding u32 offset and length spans, and Node::path is still a BString — an individually heap-allocated buffer per node — so what landed was the shellpath and duplicate-key parts, not the arena. Four hundred thousand separate allocations walked in traversal order is exactly the shape that degrades with size while a contiguous arena would not, so confirm that reading of what shipped before proposing work, then judge the arena against measured miss rates rather than against the assumption. Depends on ronin-large-manifest-fixture: without a suite workload at this size there is nothing to hold the result against, and the ad-hoc fixtures used here are not reproducible."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:50:01.932488603Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Hypothesis confirmed by hardware counters, and the conclusion is stronger than the node assumed. Measured at 100,000 statements through the commands tool, so parse and evaluate with no stats or execution, ronin against C samurai: instructions 1,363,997,067 against 1,879,756,528, cycles 1,380,993,512 against 1,196,310,697, instructions per cycle 0.99 against 1.57, cache references 45,109,338 against 32,020,494, cache misses 7,474,003 at 16.57% against 3,824,462 at 11.94%, and data TLB load misses 1,683,701 against 648,838. Ronin therefore executes 27% fewer instructions than the C reference and still spends 15% more cycles. This is not a work problem and no amount of further instruction-count optimisation addresses it — the entire deficit is memory stalls, at twice the cache miss count and 2.6 times the TLB miss count. The TLB ratio is the most diagnostic number: it says ronin's working set is spread over far more pages than samurai's for the same graph, which is what four hundred thousand separately heap-allocated Node::path buffers produce against contiguous storage. That makes the byte arena from ronin-path-interning-arena's abandoned endgame — nodes holding u32 offset and length into one append-only buffer — evidence-backed rather than speculative, and it is now the highest-value structural change available. Scope for whoever takes it: 82 non-test uses of .path, the accessor changing from returning &BString to a span lookup; Node::shellpath, already Option<BString>, should follow into the same arena since it holds the same bytes for the quoted minority; and NodeIndex probes compare against nodes[id].path, so its comparison moves to the arena too. Judge the result on cycles, IPC and TLB misses rather than on instruction count, which this measurement shows can move in the opposite direction to the truth. Note also what this says about the earlier ronin-memchr-scanner and ronin-streamed-path-scanning rejections: both were rejected on instruction counts at fixture scale, and instruction count is now demonstrably the wrong arbiter for anything touching how memory is laid out."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T12:20:26.939456587Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Profiled by cycles and by misses at 100,000 statements, and the result both localises the stalls precisely and settles whether the previously rejected scanner nodes deserve another look. By cycles: mknode_bytes 18.32%, mknode 15.90%, wide_hash 15.21%, scanstring 12.02%, memcmp 7.46%. The path-interning cluster — mknode_bytes, mknode, wide_hash and memcmp together — is 56.9% of cycles. By cache misses the same four are 80.2%: wide_hash 30.74%, mknode 19.71%, mknode_bytes 15.41%, memcmp 14.29%. By data TLB load misses they are 80.9%: wide_hash 29.79%, memcmp 18.09%, mknode_bytes 16.89%, mknode 16.09%. The mechanism is now legible end to end. Interning a path hashes its bytes, probes the open-addressed table, follows a NodeId into the node arena, and memcmps against that node's path — and every one of those steps dereferences a separately heap-allocated BString sitting at an unrelated address, so the hash reads scattered memory, the comparison reads scattered memory, and the probe loop chases pointers between them. That is why wide_hash tops the miss profile while sitting mid-table by instructions. A contiguous byte arena makes all four touch one sequential region, which is exactly the endgame ronin-path-interning-arena described and did not deliver. Compare against the instruction profile to see how badly that arbiter misleads here: by instructions append_variable is 7.19% and canonpath 4.12%, by cycles they are 2.18% and 1.81%, while wide_hash goes the other way, 7.44% by instructions against 15.21% by cycles and 30.74% of misses. On the rejected nodes: ronin-memchr-scanner and ronin-streamed-path-scanning both targeted scanstring, which is 12.02% of cycles but only 1.28% of cache misses and 1.33% of TLB misses — compute-bound, not stall-bound. Their rejections were reached against an arbiter now known to be unreliable in general, but re-implementing them is not warranted, because the profile at scale shows the scanner is simply not where the memory problem lives. Neither needs re-measuring; both should be left rejected, and this note is the reason."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T12:31:38.434727113Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-path-byte-arena
                        title "Store every node path in one contiguous arena"
                        priority 0
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T12:32:14.357892466Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T12:51:03.292321727Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Implemented and kept. Node::path and Node::shellpath are now PathSpan, a u32 offset and length into Graph::paths, a single append-only byte arena; Graph::node_path and node_shellpath resolve them, NodeIndex probes compare against arena bytes, and mknode_bytes appends directly so interning a new node allocates nothing at all rather than building a BString per path. node_shellpath now returns the plain path when quoting changes nothing, which removed the Option from every caller. Measured at 100,000 statements with both binaries built on the same compiler: cycles 1,350,421,418 to 1,318,211,555 (−2.4%), cache misses 8,124,375 to 7,303,211 (−10.1%), dTLB load misses 1,740,942 to 1,591,786 (−8.6%), instructions −4.8%. Wall clock, interleaved minimum-of-15 over four rounds, −14.4, −13.3, −5.8 and −13.9 percent, and against C samurai 0.91x, 0.96x, 0.96x and 0.90x — so at 100,000 edges ronin is now faster than the C reference, where the pre-arena binary sat at roughly parity. The wall gain exceeds what the counters alone predict and the host was not fully quiet (load 5 to 8), so treat 12% as the honest figure and the counters as the reliable direction. The prediction was wrong in size and the reason is worth keeping. This node was justified on the finding that four functions carry 80% of cache and TLB misses, and the expectation was that making their storage contiguous would remove most of that; it removed a tenth. Contiguous storage is not the same as local access. Interning probes in hash order, which is random with respect to arena order, so after the change the hash still reads a scattered offset, the probe still walks a random slot chain, and nodes is still indexed randomly — the arena removed one pointer indirection per path and the per-node allocation, but left the access pattern exactly as random as it was over a working set that still exceeds cache. Closing the remaining gap to samurai's 4.26M misses and 0.72M TLB misses is a different problem: it needs the probe sequence itself to become local, not the bytes it lands on. 368 tests pass, clippy clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T12:53:40.114685347Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-index-probe-locality
                        title "Reject index probes without dereferencing the graph"
                        priority 0
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T13:37:54.396324853Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T13:40:33.384544569Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it is the change the path arena was expected to be. Each index slot now carries a 32-bit fingerprint alongside its identifier, drawn from the high half of the path hash — bits the slot index does not use, since an index-derived fingerprint would agree across a whole probe run and filter nothing. A probe compares the fingerprint before touching the graph, so a slot that merely collided is rejected from the slot array alone. Correctness is unaffected: the fingerprint is a filter, never a decision, and a match still confirms by comparing the bytes. Measured at 100,000 statements against the immediately preceding commit: cycles 1,242,059,039 to 1,150,207,117 (−7.4%), cache misses 7,163,992 to 5,206,230 (−27.3%), dTLB load misses 1,567,400 to 890,950 (−43.2%), instructions −0.6%. Wall clock, interleaved minimum-of-12 over three rounds, −11.9, −12.4 and −10.2 percent, and against C samurai 0.84x, 0.82x and 0.82x. Ronin now spends fewer cycles than samurai as well as fewer instructions — 1.150 billion against 1.201 — where before the arena it spent more of both metrics' worth of stalling. Relative to samurai the miss gap closed from 1.76x to 1.28x on cache misses and from 2.24x to 1.27x on TLB misses. The contrast with the arena is the lesson worth keeping. The arena made the path bytes contiguous and moved misses by a tenth, because storage layout was never the problem; the probe still dereferenced nodes and then paths for every colliding slot, which is two dependent random reads per rejection at a three-quarters load factor where most probe steps are rejections. The fingerprint does not make any single access more local — it removes the accesses. Locality work should target the number of dependent random reads on the hot path, not the arrangement of what they land on. Remaining knobs if this is revisited: the load factor is still three-quarters, and dropping it to one-half would shorten probe runs further at the cost of doubling the slot array, which is now eight bytes per slot rather than four. 368 tests pass, clippy clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T13:40:58.539465917Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-index-growth-rehash
                        title "Grow the node index without rehashing paths"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:08:23.184995185Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:10:01.548382801Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it came out of profiling the load-factor knob that ronin-index-probe-locality left behind. The knob turned out to be the wrong question, and answering it properly exposed a far larger cost sitting next to it.\n\nThe load factor was measured directly, four variants (1/2, 5/8, 3/4, 7/8) built from the same tree and compared at 25,000, 60,000, 100,000 and 150,000 statements, randomised order, with two copies of the identical binary included as an inline noise control. Two things emerged. First, the noise floor is about 3% — the two identical binaries read 239.32 and 245.96 ms at minimum-of-25 — so anything under that is not a result. Second, and this is the real point, the load factor is not a continuous knob at all. The table is a power of two, so the load factor only changes behaviour where it changes which power of two the node count lands on. At 100,000 statements (200,001 nodes) the 1/2, 5/8 and 3/4 variants all finish on a 524,288-slot table at 38% occupancy — bit-identical final state, differing only in how full the intermediate tables got during the fill — while 7/8 finishes on 262,144 slots at 76%, half the memory and one fewer doubling. That made 7/8 15% faster at 100,000 and 12% slower at 60,000, where it saves no doubling and only lengthens probe runs. A knob whose sign flips with graph size is not a knob worth tuning.\n\nWhat the sweep actually pointed at was the doubling. Instrumenting grow showed 15 of them for a 100,000-statement manifest, rehashing 24, 48, 96 … 196,608 entries — 393,192 rehashes in total, against 200,001 nodes finally stored. Growth was re-hashing nearly twice as many entries as the table ends up holding, and each rehash was precisely the dependent-random-read pattern the fingerprint had just removed from lookups: a random read into `nodes` for the span, a random read into `paths` for the bytes, and a hash of those bytes. The fingerprint fixed the lookup path and left the growth path doing the same thing 393,192 times.\n\nCeiling first, to size the prize before designing: a build with INITIAL_SLOTS pinned to 524,288 so no growth ever happens ran cycles 1,014,381,980 to 822,008,894, cache misses 5,048,456 to 2,252,651, dTLB load misses 860,399 to 431,380, wall minimum 238.19 to 197.52 ms. So growth was roughly a sixth of the entire parse-and-evaluate run and over half of all misses.\n\nThe fix keeps the slot at eight bytes and needs no size estimate. The slot now stores the low half of the path hash rather than the high half. The high half filtered marginally better, but the low half is the half the slot index is drawn from, so growth can place an entry by masking a value it has already read — no hash, no arena access, no touching the graph at all. Placement order does not matter, because every entry still lands in the first vacancy at or after its home slot, which is the whole invariant linear probing requires. The filtering given up is small: the index consumes log2(slots) of the thirty-two bits, leaving thirteen at a 524,288-slot table, so all but one collision in eight thousand is still rejected from the slot array, and a false positive merely costs the byte comparison that used to happen unconditionally.\n\nMeasured at 100,000 statements against the immediately preceding commit: cycles 1,014,381,980 to 786,076,050 (−22.5%), instructions 1,271,604,529 to 1,207,566,990 (−5.0%), cache misses 5,048,456 to 2,603,195 (−48.4%), dTLB load misses 860,399 to 373,127 (−56.6%), wall minimum-of-20 238.19 to 196.17 ms (−17.6%). That matches the presize ceiling — 196.17 against 197.52 — so the growth cost is gone rather than reduced, and without the manifest-size heuristic presizing would have needed. On the suite, large-manifest-parse median 292.113 to 212.524 ms against C samurai's 312.929, which is 0.68x where it was 0.89x.\n\nWith growth cheap, the load factor was re-measured on top of the change, same four variants rebuilt on the new base. The spread collapses into the noise floor and stops ranking consistently: 7/8 best at 25,000 and 100,000, 1/2 best at 60,000, 3/4 best at 150,000, with the full range 1.4% to 5% at every size. Keep 3/4. The knob was only ever a proxy for growth cost, and with that paid directly it is worth nothing.\n\nThe lesson generalises the one from ronin-index-probe-locality rather than repeating it. That node found the cost in the dependent random reads on the lookup path; this one found the same reads on the maintenance path, where nobody had looked because growth is amortised and amortised is usually read as cheap. It is not cheap when each amortised unit is a cache miss and a TLB miss. 369 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-stat-directory-contention
                        title "Split stat chunks on directory boundaries"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:21:43.302893344Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:22:10.180376639Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done. This is the first of the three remaining workloads where ronin trailed C samurai, and the cause turned out to be a regression this programme introduced two nodes ago rather than anything inherited from the port.\n\ndeep-graph-evaluation is a chain of phony edges — `build node/i: phony node/i-1` — so every path lives in one directory and none of them exists on disk. Profiled at 50,000 links, ronin burned 967,749,907 cycles against samurai's 473,366,676 while executing *fewer* instructions, 563,005,026 against 584,084,925. Instructions per cycle 0.58 against 1.23. Cache misses were only 84% higher and branch misses were identical to three digits, so neither accounts for a doubling of cycles. The symbol profile did: 45.53% of the run in `__pv_queued_spin_lock_slowpath`, plus another 9.5% across `__raw_callee_save___pv_queued_spin_unlock` and `_raw_spin_lock`. Half the run was kernel spinlock contention, and the contending threads were ours — the batched stat fan-out from ronin-stat-batch-cpu-cost.\n\nThe mechanism is negative dentry instantiation. A lookup that finds a file can be served from the dentry cache under RCU with no exclusive lock. A lookup that finds nothing has to record that absence in the parent directory, which takes that directory's lock. Four threads looking up absent names in the *same* directory therefore serialize on one lock and pay the contention on top. Isolated cleanly by taking the identical 50,000-path single-directory fixture and touching every file so the paths exist: with the files present, fanning out to four costs 148.49 ms of task-clock against 108.85 serial, +36%, the ordinary price of parallelism; with the same paths absent it costs 270.14 against 115.07, +135%, and cycles go 1,052,470,227 against 451,937,109. Same paths, same directory, same thread count — presence alone is worth a factor of two.\n\nThat case is not exotic. Every output of a build that has not run yet is an absent path, so this is the first build of any tree, and the clean-tree fixture that motivated the fan-out never showed it because its files all exist by construction. Both fixtures were needed to see the shape; either alone gives the wrong answer, which is the same lesson the clean-tree node recorded from the other direction.\n\nThe fix keeps the fan-out and removes the sharing: chunk boundaries now slide forward from the ideal split to the next point where the parent directory changes, so one directory is one worker's business. Cost is one pass comparing adjacent parents as raw `OsStr` bytes rather than as `Path` components, which is nothing against a syscall each. A graph spread over many directories still fans out; a graph confined to one produces no split at all and runs on the calling thread, which is where it belongs. The remainder chunk deliberately runs inline rather than spawning, so the single-directory case starts no threads whatsoever.\n\nMeasured at 50,000 links: task-clock 269.94 to 111.01 ms against a serial build's 111.28 and samurai's 114.01 — contention gone, not reduced, and now marginally ahead of the C reference on CPU. Wall median 124.162 to 118.932 against samurai's 113.606. At the suite's 2,000 links, wall median 6.706 to 5.992 against samurai's 5.601, so the gap closes from 1.20x to 1.07x. Crucially the clean-tree win is untouched, because alternating `out/` and `src/` paths mean a directory change at nearly every index and the split still lands where it used to: at 20,000 edges, 57.793 to 57.502 ms median against samurai's 91.472, and at the suite's 2,000, 7.712 to 7.451 against 9.550.\n\nWhat remains on this workload is roughly 7% at suite size and 5% at 50,000, which at 2.2 microseconds per stat is the syscall itself rather than anything above it. 370 tests pass including a new one asserting a chunk never splits a directory, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-startup-floor
                        title "Trace the small-workload gap to process startup"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:35:39.247658116Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:36:08.667024156Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, with a small change shipped, a large one measured and rejected, and the workload's name shown to be misleading.\n\ndependency-log-load was the second of the three workloads behind C samurai, at 3.666 ms against 2.937. The gap has nothing to do with dependency logs. Scaled to 10,000 edges the ordering reverses completely — ronin 37.589 ms median against samurai's 54.307, so ronin is 1.44x *faster* at loading a real dependency log, with cycles 213,433,927 against 206,555,603 and instructions 268,892,314 against 252,261,154, near enough parity on work. The suite fixture is 300 edges and the whole run is under three milliseconds, so what it measures is mostly the cost of existing.\n\nMeasured directly. An empty manifest — one phony edge, nothing to do — runs in 0.950 ms minimum for ronin against 0.628 for samurai. That 0.32 ms is the entire dependency-log deficit and then some: subtract each tool's own floor from its 300-edge run and the remaining work costs ronin 1.558 ms against samurai's 1.540. Ronin is at parity on the workload and 0.32 ms behind on starting up.\n\nWhere the floor goes, from a startup ladder: /bin/true 0.561 ms, a `rustc -O` hello world 0.688, samurai --version 0.583, ronin --version 0.808. So roughly 0.13 ms is what a Rust binary costs over the smallest possible process, and 0.12 ms is ronin's own doing, against samurai which sits essentially on the /bin/true floor. Ronin executes 1,745,579 instructions and takes 105 page faults to start and do nothing, against samurai's 827,054 and 74. A timestamped syscall trace puts the ronin-specific portion in three places: signal handler installation for HUP, INT, QUIT and TERM (eight `rt_sigaction` and four `sendto` through signal-hook's self-pipe), the poller, eventfd and timerfd built for a supervisor that in a no-op build never runs a job, and a walk of `/proc/self/cgroup` plus four levels of `cpu.max` to answer `available_parallelism`.\n\nShipped: `available_parallelism` is now resolved once into a `OnceLock`, and `stat_many` derives its thread count from the work before consulting the machine at all, so a scan too small to split never asks. The standard library does not cache this — measured in isolation it is 51 to 101 microseconds on the first call and still 30 to 45 on subsequent ones, because every call re-reads the cgroup hierarchy. The syscall count confirms the fix: cgroup-related opens per run drop from 12 to 6. The honest caveat is that the wall-clock effect is inside the noise floor, because those files are in page cache and each read costs a few microseconds rather than the tens the cold measurement suggested. Kept because it is strictly less work with no cost, not because it was measurable.\n\nRejected: static linking, which works and is not worth it. Adding `-C target-feature=+crt-static` for linux-gnu removes the dynamic loader's resolution of libc and libgcc and is decisive on startup — empty manifest 0.928 to 0.633 ms minimum against samurai's 0.654, so ronin starts faster than the C tool. Every small workload followed: dependency-log 3.129 to 2.711 against samurai's 2.667, deep-graph 5.851 to 5.372 against 5.323, clean-tree 7.691 to 7.111. Both remaining small gaps closed to parity, for one config file. Then the performance gate refused it: scheduler-barrier regressed to 1.47x from a recorded 1.00x. Measured directly, 42.406 ms median to 70.182 — a 65% regression on the workload that exists to measure job spawning. The trace shows why: `std::process::Command` goes through glibc's `posix_spawn`, which in the dynamic build issues `clone3` with the vfork-style flags and in the static build falls back to plain `clone`, so every one of the 128 spawns copies page tables instead of sharing them. Trading 0.3 ms of startup for 28 ms of spawn cost is exactly the wrong way round for a tool whose purpose is spawning compilers, so it was reverted. The gate caught this, which is the second time this programme has been saved by a workload nobody was looking at — worth remembering when the next global build-configuration change looks free.\n\nWhat remains on this workload is around 0.3 ms of process and runtime startup, most of it not ours: dynamic loading of two shared objects and Rust runtime initialisation. Closing it needs a smaller or differently linked binary, and the one available lever is now known to cost more elsewhere than it saves here. 370 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T14:18:24.862446061Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Superseded in part: the static-linking rejection recorded above was reversed by ronin-static-startup, which shipped it. The measurement behind the rejection was sound — static linking really did cost 65% on scheduler-barrier — but the cause was not chased, and it was ours. Ronin set a `cwd` on every child that the child already had; that makes Rust's `Command` reach for the weak `posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np`, and a static link does not supply it, so every spawn fell back from `posix_spawn` to `fork`. Removing the redundant `cwd` removes the fallback, after which static and dynamic spawn identically and the startup win is free. The diagnosis in this node — that the workload's deficit is process startup rather than dependency logs — still stands, and is what made the second attempt worth making."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-dispatch-syscall-cost
                        title "Cut avoidable syscalls off the job dispatch loop"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:48:11.699444839Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T14:48:38.0144385Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Partly closed, and precisely diagnosed where the rest sits. This is the third and last of the workloads behind C samurai.\n\nscheduler-barrier runs 128 `touch` edges at -j 8, so it measures dispatch and nothing else. CPU is not the difference: over five reset runs each, ronin averages 291 million instructions and 713 million cycles against samurai's 285 million and 697 million, and task-clock 291 ms against 287. Identical work. A cycle profile of the parent finds nothing above 2.1%, all of it kernel. The deficit is latency, not computation, and it showed up as occupancy: reconstructing in-flight children from the parent's own clone3 and wait4 timestamps, ronin averages 5.71 concurrent jobs against a target of 8 while samurai averages 6.12, and ronin sits at four in flight for 14.5% of the run where samurai does so for 1.7%. It is not keeping the pipe full, because each refill costs more.\n\nTracing one full job cycle in the parent, from spawn to the next spawn, gave the itemised bill: ronin issues 33 syscalls where samurai issues 23. Two of the extras were pure waste and are now gone.\n\nThe first was `create_dir_all` per output. On a directory that already exists it still costs a failing `mkdir` plus a `statx` to find out what failed, and a build calls it once for every output of every edge — so on any tree whose outputs share a directory, which is every tree, that is two syscalls between one job finishing and the next starting, repeated for the whole build. The disk interface now remembers which directories it has ensured. The cache is per-interface and behind a mutex because the type is shared and must stay `Sync`, though in practice only the single-threaded dispatch loop touches it; the same caveat Ninja's equivalent cache carries applies here, namely that a directory deleted mid-build will not be recreated.\n\nThe second was `Stdio::null()`, which opens `/dev/null` afresh for every spawn and so puts a path resolution on the dispatch loop once per job. `/dev/null` is now opened once for the process and duplicated per child, which is a `dup` instead of an `openat`, with a fallback to `Stdio::null()` if anything goes wrong — a failure there costs exactly the open this exists to avoid, so it is not worth reporting.\n\nMeasured, minimum-of-many with the output tree and log reset before every run: at 128 jobs, median 40.951 to 39.510 ms against samurai's 35.890, and at 1,024 jobs, 308.391 to 298.426 against 274.268. So 3.2 to 3.5 percent, and the ratio moves from 1.14x to 1.09x. Confirming the mechanism rather than just the number, the window from a child's SIGCHLD to the next clone3 now measures 1.501 ms median against samurai's 1.654 under identical tracing, where before the change ronin was the slower of the two. The dispatch path itself is no longer behind.\n\nWhat remains is seven extra parent syscalls per job, and five of them belong to the `polling` crate rather than to us: it re-arms with `EPOLLONESHOT`, so every `wait` costs a `timerfd_settime` to disarm the timeout plus two `epoll_ctl` re-arms of the timer and the notifier, on top of the `epoll_ctl` add and delete that each child genuinely needs. Samurai uses a bare `poll` over its pipe set and registers nothing. Closing that means driving epoll directly — level-triggered, no oneshot, no timerfd, passing the timeout to `epoll_wait` itself — which would remove three syscalls per job and two file descriptors at startup, worth perhaps four percent here. It is deliberately not done in this node: the evented supervisor is recent, its poller is entangled with signal wake-up and interrupt delivery, and that is conformance-visible surface. The remaining two extras are an `ioctl(FIONBIO)` to make only the read end of the output socketpair non-blocking, which cannot be folded into the `socketpair` call without also making the child's stdout non-blocking, and one extra `prlimit64` inside glibc's `posix_spawn`. Neither is ours to remove.\n\n370 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-static-startup
                        title "Stop paying the dynamic loader on every invocation"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T14:16:56.048668119Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T14:17:19.941140761Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it reverses a rejection recorded a few hours earlier in ronin-startup-floor. That node measured static linking as decisive on startup and then rejected it because the performance gate caught it regressing scheduler-barrier by 65%. The rejection was correct on the evidence available; what was missing was the cause, and once found it turned out to be ours and removable.\n\nThe cause is a weak symbol. Rust's `Command` can only use `posix_spawn` when it can resolve `posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np`, which it looks up weakly; a static link does not supply it, so `nm` shows it present but undefined and the standard library silently falls back to `fork` and `exec`. That is page tables copied per spawn instead of shared: measured at 128 jobs, `clone` at 127 microseconds a call against `clone3` at 74. The fallback only triggers because ronin was setting a `cwd` on every child — and it was setting one that every child already had.\n\nThe library deliberately lets several runners share a process with different roots, which is why a child's directory is set per spawn rather than by moving the process; that is what keeps tests isolated. But the binary's root comes from `Runner::from_process`, so outside a `-C` it *is* the process's own directory, and imposing it is asking for what is already true. `directory_to_impose` now compares the two and returns an empty path when they match, which is every invocation from a shell. A runner rooted elsewhere still moves its children, so isolation is untouched, and `-C` still imposes a directory and still takes the slow spawn path under a static link — correct, and rare enough to leave.\n\nWith that in place static linking is no longer a trade. Spawn-heavy work is indistinguishable between the two: 1,024 jobs at -j 8, median 320.686 ms dynamic against 321.287 static; 128 jobs, 41.456 against 40.614. The syscall confirms it directly — the static build issues `clone3`, at 46 microseconds a call against the dynamic build's 65, because a static image has fewer mappings to set up.\n\nWhat it buys is the whole startup floor, on every invocation of every workload. Empty manifest 1.372 to 0.980 ms median against C samurai's 0.962. dependency-log-load 3.184 to 2.578 against samurai's 2.622 — the gap that node traced entirely to startup is closed and reversed, and the full gate agrees at 3.468 against 3.995. deep-graph 5.957 to 5.814 against 5.422. clean-tree 7.919 to 7.480, widening a lead. Peak RSS falls rather than rises, by 15 to 30 percent across the catalog. The binary grows about 70%, which for a tool run thousands of times a day is the right way round.\n\nTwo caveats worth carrying. The config lives outside the published crate, so `cargo install ronin` builds dynamically and gets the startup cost back; anyone shipping a package should apply the same flag. And the two halves are load-bearing together — remove the `directory_to_impose` check and static linking silently costs 65% on spawning, remove the static link and the check does nothing measurable. Both files say so.\n\nThe methodological point is worth more than the milliseconds. The gate rejected this change on a real measurement, and the right response to a real measurement is not always to accept the verdict — it is to find the mechanism. Here the mechanism was a fallback nobody had asked for, triggered by a redundant argument, and once named it cost one function to remove. A rejection with a number but no mechanism is provisional. 371 tests pass including one asserting a child is only moved to a directory it is not already in, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-musl-static-build
                        title "Measure a static musl build against static glibc"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T14:25:15.810716904Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T14:25:37.396937561Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Rejected on measurement. musl wins the thing ronin-static-startup was chasing and loses everything else by more.\n\nBuilt for x86_64-unknown-linux-musl, which is static by default so the crt-static flag in .cargo/config.toml does not apply to it. The binary is 1,555,000 bytes against static glibc's 2,462,792, a 37% saving, and it spawns correctly: musl's `posix_spawn` issues `clone` with CLONE_VM and CLONE_VFORK rather than glibc's `clone3`, which is the same address-space-sharing behaviour and not the page-table-copying fork that the weak-symbol fallback produced, at 56 microseconds a call against static glibc's 46.\n\nOn startup it is the best of the three. Empty manifest, minimum-of-51 interleaved: musl 0.676 ms minimum and 0.997 median, static glibc 0.777 and 1.085, C samurai 0.678 and 0.998. So musl lands exactly on samurai's floor and takes another 8% off what static glibc achieved.\n\nThen it gives all of that back many times over on anything that does work. Instruction counts settle it independently of machine load, which matters because a large external rustc build pushed load average to 31 during this session and inflated every wall figure taken here. Parsing and evaluating 100,000 statements: musl 1,429,671,885 instructions against static glibc's 1,205,457,312, up 18.6%, and 1,120,134,608 cycles against 862,228,746, up 29.9%. Wall ratios reproduced consistently across two very different load levels — deep-graph +31% and +32%, the 25,000-statement parse +24% and +21% — so the ordering is not a load artifact even though the absolute milliseconds are.\n\nThe symbol profile names both causes. musl spends 5.12% of the run in `__libc_malloc_impl` and 3.13% in `__libc_free`, so 8.25% in the allocator; on static glibc neither appears above the 2% reporting threshold at all. And musl spends 7.86% in `memcpy` plus 6.88% in `memmove`, 14.74% together, where static glibc spends 3.96% in `__memmove_avx_unaligned_erms` — glibc keeps its IFUNC dispatch to AVX2 and ERMS string routines even when statically linked, and musl's portable implementations are roughly 3.7 times the share of the run. A workload built on path interning, arena appends and string building moves a great many bytes, so that difference is not incidental to it.\n\nNet: musl buys about 0.09 ms of startup and costs 11 to 31 percent of everything above it. Wrong trade, and not a close one. Static glibc stays.\n\nTwo things worth keeping from this. First, the glibc allocator is not a bottleneck here — it does not register in the profile — so the recurring temptation to swap in mimalloc or jemalloc has no evidence behind it on this workload and should not be attempted without some. Second, the reason musl loses is largely IFUNC-dispatched string routines, which is a good reminder that \"statically linked\" does not mean \"loses the optimised libc\": static glibc keeps them, and that is a large part of why the ronin-static-startup change was free rather than a compromise.\n\nNo repository change: the musl target was installed for the experiment and nothing in the tree depends on it."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-mimalloc-allocator
                        title "Measure mimalloc against the system allocator on both libcs"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T18:12:29.990935447Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T18:12:57.603226938Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Rejected, but the hypothesis behind it was right and the previous node's dismissal of it was too broad. ronin-musl-static-build recorded that \"the recurring temptation to swap in mimalloc or jemalloc has no evidence behind it on this workload\" — that was true of glibc, where the allocator does not register in the profile, and false of musl, where it was 8.25%. Measuring both was the correct response.\n\nmimalloc was wired in as `#[global_allocator]` in the binary only, so the library keeps the system allocator for its consumers and the allocation-accounting harness, which wraps `System` in its own counting allocator in a separate example binary, is unaffected. Four builds were compared: glibc and musl, each with and without mimalloc. An external rustc build held load average between 30 and 40 for this entire measurement, so wall clock is corroborative only and the conclusion rests on instruction counts and page faults, which are load-independent.\n\nOn musl mimalloc does exactly what was predicted. `__libc_malloc_impl` and `__libc_free` disappear from the profile completely — they were 5.12% and 3.13% — and no mimalloc symbol replaces them above the reporting threshold. Instructions fall at every size above trivial: deps300 11,684,172 to 9,461,016 (−19%), deep-graph 32,610,559 to 25,266,902 (−22%), clean-tree 49,082,576 to 43,915,760 (−11%), 25,000-statement parse 360,354,050 to 310,185,705 (−14%), 100,000-statement parse 1,438,341,278 to 1,193,193,091 (−17%). That takes musl from 18 to 33 percent worse than glibc to roughly level with it, and at 100,000 statements slightly ahead — 1,193,193,091 against glibc's 1,218,753,578.\n\nIt still does not win, for two reasons that are not the allocator. First, musl's string routines remain untouched: memcpy plus memmove are 16.46% of the musl+mimalloc profile against 8.87% for glibc+mimalloc's IFUNC-dispatched `__memmove_avx_unaligned_erms` and `__memmove_avx_unaligned`. Second, mimalloc trades instructions for pages, and pages are not free. Page faults on the 25,000-statement parse go 7,526 to 10,293 on musl and 7,691 to 10,311 on glibc, about +35% in both cases, and that shows up in wall clock even where instructions fell: musl+mimalloc parses 25,000 statements in 117.485 ms minimum against plain musl's 110.933, despite executing 14% fewer instructions.\n\nThe page cost lands hardest exactly where this programme has spent the day trying to win. On an empty manifest mimalloc adds 61% to glibc's instruction count (1,117,251 to 1,797,816) and 42% to musl's (1,089,850 to 1,547,638), and roughly 130% to page faults in both — 68 to 156, and 57 to 137. Wall minimum on that fixture, under load: glibc 1.952 ms, glibc+mimalloc 2.560, musl 1.845, musl+mimalloc 2.152, samurai 1.868. mimalloc gives back a large fraction of what ronin-static-startup just bought, on every invocation, and a build tool's most common invocation is the one with nothing to do.\n\nOn glibc it is a straightforward loss. Instructions move +0.6% to +6.8% at every size except the 100,000-statement parse, where they fall 4.2%; page faults rise 35%; and two new symbols appear in the profile, `mi_free` at 2.77% and `__pv_queued_spin_lock_slowpath` at 2.67%, the latter suggesting mimalloc's arena management is contending in the kernel. Wall under load agrees at every size measured: deep-graph 8.349 ms minimum against 10.294, the 25,000-statement parse 88.562 against 102.611.\n\nReverted; the tree is byte-identical to before the experiment. glibc with the system allocator remains the shipped configuration, and it is the best of the four at every size measured except the 100,000-statement instruction count.\n\nTwo things worth keeping. First, musl+mimalloc is now a *viable* configuration rather than a bad one — roughly glibc parity on instructions, in a binary of 1,721,696 bytes against glibc+mimalloc's 2,625,840 — so if full-static portability or binary size ever becomes the goal, that combination is the one to reach for and this node is the measurement. Second, the general lesson: an allocator that reduces instruction count can still lose on wall clock by faulting in more pages, so allocator swaps must be judged on page faults and wall clock, never on instructions or allocation counts alone."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T18:25:21.373205216Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Clean-machine numbers landed (load 3.78 at start, 3.63 at finish, against 30-40 for the measurement above). They confirm the rejection and correct one claim in it that was wrong in the way this programme keeps getting caught.\n\nThe correction first. The entry above says musl+mimalloc is \"roughly glibc parity\" and offers it as the configuration to reach for if static portability or binary size ever matters. That was inferred from instruction counts, and instruction counts are exactly the arbiter this programme has now been burned by three times. On a quiet machine musl+mimalloc parses 100,000 statements in 236.606 ms minimum against glibc's 191.029 — 24% slower, not parity — while executing *fewer* instructions, 1,188,444,945 against 1,204,504,324. Cycles explain it: 1,005,553,198 against 838,134,125, so instructions per cycle are 1.18 against 1.44. musl+mimalloc does less work and takes 20% more cycles to do it, because musl's portable memcpy and memmove move fewer bytes per instruction than glibc's AVX routines and mimalloc faults in more pages. The gap holds at every real size: 25,000-statement parse 61.911 against 46.976 (+32%), clean-tree at 20,000 edges 61.406 against 53.271 (+15%). It is not parity and it is not close, and the offer to ship it should be read with that correction attached — it would cost 15 to 32 percent on anything that parses.\n\nEverything else stands and is sharper. On glibc, mimalloc loses on wall clock at every size despite fewer instructions at scale: 100,000-statement parse 199.336 ms minimum against 191.029, 25,000 against 46.976 at 52.115, clean-tree 59.300 against 53.271, deep-graph 5.228 against 4.945. At 100,000 statements it executes 3.8% fewer instructions (1,158,537,536) for 1.9% more cycles (854,155,123) and 12% more cache misses. On musl it does help — 100,000-statement parse 248.024 to 236.606, deep-graph 6.392 to 5.197 — but never enough to reach glibc. And it damages startup on both, which is the measurement that matters most for a tool usually invoked with nothing to do: empty manifest minimum 0.665 to 0.969 ms on glibc and 0.652 to 0.789 on musl.\n\nOne genuinely new result the loaded run had obscured: plain musl has the best startup of anything measured, including C samurai. Empty manifest, minimum-of-51 interleaved: musl 0.652 ms minimum and 0.815 median, glibc 0.665 and 0.916, samurai 0.674 and 0.917. So musl is 11% better than both on median startup. It is still the wrong choice overall because it gives that back many times over on work, but the startup floor is not where its problem lies, and if a future change ever makes parsing cheap enough that startup dominates the catalog, this number is why musl would deserve re-measuring.\n\nJob dispatch is indifferent to all of it, as expected of a workload that is neither allocating nor copying: 128 jobs at -j 8, minimums of 38.676, 38.245, 37.699 and 37.662 ms across the four builds against samurai's 35.955.\n\nShipped configuration is unchanged: glibc with the system allocator, which wins every workload here that does any work. The standing lesson is now stated three ways in this plan and should be taken as settled — instruction count is not a proxy for time. Judge allocator and libc changes on cycles, page faults and wall clock."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-scanner-bulk-runs
                        title "Cross runs of ordinary bytes in one step"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T19:10:55.421626119Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T19:11:24.647749086Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it reverses two earlier rejections and one answer I gave on the first question of this whole programme.\n\n`scanstring` walked one byte at a time: a bounds-checked read, a match testing up to seven alternatives, and an index bump, repeated across every path and every command in the manifest. At 100,000 statements it was the largest symbol in the profile at 15.87%. It now crosses each run of ordinary bytes in a single step, stopping only where something needs handling.\n\nWhat makes this safe rather than fiddly is that the loop kept no per-byte state at all. `advance_within_line` is exactly `column += 1; index += 1`, and a run cannot contain a line ending because a line ending would end it — so advancing by the whole run is not an approximation of the old loop, it is the same arithmetic done once. The terminator sets fall out of the match directly: a value ends at `$`, carriage return or newline; a path additionally ends at any of `:`, `|`, space or tab, with tab present so it can be rejected, since Ninja forbids one there and the scan has to stop to say so.\n\nChoosing how to search that set is where this got interesting, and the honest record is that a microbenchmark led me wrong. A standalone harness comparing byte-at-a-time, a 256-entry membership table, `bstr`'s byte-set search and three overlapping `memchr3` calls ranked them inconsistently between runs of the same binary — the byte-set search measured 17.97 ns on one run and 35.72 ns on the next for identical input, with the table moving the opposite way. Rather than tune the harness, both candidates were built into the tool and measured there. Parsing 100,000 statements: 787.9 million cycles before, 744.6 with the byte-set search, 722.6 with the table. At 25,000: 209.2, 185.3, 173.2. The table also executes the fewest instructions of the three, which the byte-set search does not, because that one rebuilds its set on every call and most runs here are short. The table wins at every size and is what shipped.\n\nOne near-miss worth recording. The clean-tree fixture appeared to regress with both variants — cycles 42.8 million to 45.0 — and it would have been easy to build a size-conditional fast path around that. It is not real: clean-tree runs the batched stat fan-out, cycles and task-clock sum across threads, and the same run reported task-clock of 22.46 against 11.11 for two builds of the same workload. Wall clock, minimum-of-41 interleaved, is 6.654 ms before and 6.664 after at 2,000 edges, and 55.530 against 55.124 at 20,000 — no change either way. This programme recorded that threading defeats summed CPU counters twice before, and it nearly caught me a third time.\n\nMeasured, on an idle machine. 100,000-statement parse: cycles 787,935,952 to 722,562,720 (−8.3%), instructions 1,204,552,749 to 1,125,280,172 (−6.6%), wall minimum-of-21 181.237 to 169.893 ms and median 203.020 to 190.875, against C samurai's 286.482 and 301.848. 25,000 statements: cycles 209,189,966 to 173,191,797 (−17.2%). Deep graph: 20,210,978 to 19,526,858 (−3.4%). `scanstring` falls from 15.87% of the profile to 6.44%. On the suite, large-manifest-parse median 190.528 ms against samurai's 317.304, which is 0.60x, and deep-graph-evaluation moves ahead of samurai for the first time — 6.702 against 7.053.\n\nThe corrections this owes. The first question asked in this programme was \"simd?\", and the answer given was that vectorised scanning was not the gap. That was true of the fixtures being measured then — 4,000 edges with ten-byte paths — and false at the scale the tool is meant for. Both `ronin-memchr-scanner` and `ronin-streamed-path-scanning` were rejected on instruction counts at that same small scale, and a later node reaffirmed the rejections on the grounds that `scanstring` was compute-bound rather than stall-bound. That reasoning was sound for a locality fix and beside the point for this one: being compute-bound is precisely the condition under which doing less work per byte pays. The lesson is narrower than \"instruction counts mislead\", which this plan already says three times — it is that a rejection is only valid at the scale and shape it was measured on, and the fixtures this programme judged the scanner against were an order of magnitude too small to see it.\n\n372 tests pass including one asserting the bulk skip leaves the column exactly where stepping would, for values, paths, tabs inside values, and a rejected tab inside a path. Clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-byte-class-tables
                        title "Classify path bytes by table, and reject arena presizing"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T19:22:22.208283217Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T19:22:46.138821636Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "This node was opened to presize the graph arenas and that idea is dead on measurement. What shipped instead is the byte-class technique from ronin-scanner-bulk-runs applied where the profile said it still paid.\n\nThe presizing premise was that growing by doubling touches about twice the memory it keeps, since each doubling allocates afresh — the kernel zeroing every new page — and copies everything so far. `clear_page_rep` was 8.4% of the run, so it looked like a target. A reservation derived from the manifest size was added for nodes, edges and the path arena, sized to cover the 100,000-statement fixture exactly. It did nothing: cycles 775.1 million to 784.6, instructions 1,130.2 million to 1,128.7, page faults 29,927 to 29,903.\n\nThe reason is `mremap`. glibc's `realloc` extends a large mapping in place, or moves it without copying pages, so a `Vec` past a few hundred kilobytes grows for the cost of one syscall and no page traffic at all. Counted directly, the unreserved build issues 19 `mremap` calls over the run and the reserved one issues 2 — the doubling was already nearly free, and had been all along. The premise was simply false for allocations this size, and reverted.\n\nTwo further things were checked before abandoning the direction, both already done. The adjacency lists are not a source of allocation: `IdVec` has been `SmallVec<[T; 4]>` since earlier in this programme, so up to four identifiers cost no allocation and no extra footprint, and `Node` is 72 bytes and `Edge` 136. And the remaining 800,227 allocation requests at 100,000 statements — 8.0 per build statement — are parse-time temporaries rather than graph structure, with `malloc` and `free` together under 3% of the profile. So `clear_page_rep` is first-touch of memory the tool genuinely writes to, and the only way to reduce it is to want less memory, not to ask for it more cleverly.\n\nWhat did pay was `shell_escape_path`, which every interned path passes through to decide whether quoting would change it. The test was `!byte.is_ascii_alphanumeric() && !b\"_+-./\".contains(byte)` — three range checks and then a *linear search of a five-byte string*, per byte, over every path in the manifest. Replaced with a 256-entry membership table, exactly as the scanner's terminator sets. Measured at 100,000 statements: cycles 773,298,348 to 751,588,888 (−2.8%), instructions 1,125,703,750 to 1,077,457,353 (−4.3%). The function has dropped out of the profile entirely, from 5.13% to below the 1.8% reporting threshold.\n\nThe profile that remains at 100,000 statements, for whoever picks this up: `NodeIndex::probe` 10.43%, `clear_page_rep` 9.53%, `scanstring` 7.98%, `node_for` 5.27%, page faults 3.44%, `memmove` 3.36%, `parse` 3.26%, `wide_hash` 3.13%, `canonpath` 3.03%, `append_variable` 3.02%, `list_del` 2.35%, `scan::space` 2.14%. The memory system is now the largest category at roughly 15% and is, per the above, not addressable by allocation strategy. `canonpath` and `scan::space` are the two remaining per-byte loops and are worth perhaps two percent between them.\n\n372 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-dispatch-residual-gap
                        title "Account for the last of the dispatch gap and stop"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T19:59:56.891630269Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T20:00:26.235751887Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Rejected before writing any code, because the estimate that motivated it was arithmetic done on the wrong trace, and the corrected number is too small to justify the change.\n\nronin-dispatch-syscall-cost proposed driving epoll directly instead of through the `polling` crate, on the grounds that its `EPOLLONESHOT` re-arm costs five syscalls per job — a `timerfd_settime` plus two `epoll_ctl` re-arms per `wait`, on top of the add and delete each child needs. That per-job figure came from a trace taken at -j 1, where every job necessarily gets its own `wait`. The suite runs at -j 8, where one `wait` serves many completions. Counted at -j 8 over 1,024 jobs: 181 `epoll_pwait` calls, 181 `timerfd_settime`, and 2,416 `epoll_ctl` of which 2,048 are the add and delete each child genuinely needs. The crate's re-arm overhead is therefore 543 syscalls across the run, about 0.53 per job rather than five — under two percent of dispatch syscalls, and dispatch is not the whole gap. Replacing a deliberately chosen dependency in the subsystem that carries interrupt delivery, for that, is not a trade worth making.\n\nHaving corrected the estimate, the remaining gap was itemised properly. Occupancy first, since it is what tracks wall clock: reconstructing in-flight children from the parent's own timestamps, ronin now averages 5.51 concurrent jobs against samurai's 5.70, a 3.3% deficit, down from 5.71 against 6.12 before the earlier dispatch fixes. Then the parent's syscalls per 128 jobs, ronin against samurai: `epoll_ctl` 302 against 0, `close` 524 against 261, `prlimit64` 770 against 641, with `socketpair` against `pipe2`, `recvfrom` against `read` and `fcntl` all level. So roughly 5.4 extra syscalls per job, which at a microsecond and a half each is about a millisecond over 128 jobs on a forty-millisecond run — 2.5%, and consistent with the measured occupancy deficit.\n\nEvery one of those is load-bearing. The 2.36 `epoll_ctl` are the price of registering each child with an event loop, which samurai does not pay because it uses `poll` over a small array; that is the accepted cost of ronin-evented-process-supervision, whose point was never poll versus epoll but not spawning a thread per child. The 2.05 extra `close` calls are the price of a socketpair's two ends plus a duplicated writer for stderr plus a duplicated `/dev/null`, against a pipe's two — and the stderr duplicate cannot go, because `Command` needs two owned descriptors and the alternative, a `pre_exec` closure, would cost the whole `posix_spawn` fast path. The extra `prlimit64` is inside glibc's `posix_spawn` and is not ours.\n\nThe one genuinely removable syscall is the `epoll_ctl` delete before close, since closing a descriptor removes it from the epoll set on Linux. That is about one per job, perhaps half a percent, and it would cost a `#[cfg(target_os = \"linux\")]` split in the supervisor plus a dependence on kernel behaviour that fails silently the moment any descriptor is duplicated. Not worth it.\n\nOne hypothesis was tested and did not hold. If the evented design's registration cost is real, it should be repaid at high job counts where `poll` degrades. Measured on 1,024 jobs at -j 8, -j 32 and -j 128, ronin runs 348.2, 351.3 and 350.0 ms minimum against samurai's 322.8, 316.4 and 317.7 — the gap is flat at eight to ten percent and neither tool improves past -j 8. The fixture cannot show the difference either way, because 1,024 invocations of `touch` are bound by process spawn rather than event dispatch. Demonstrating the evented design's benefit would need a workload with many concurrent long-lived children producing output, which the catalog does not have and which would be artificial to construct. The design's actual justification stands on its own and is already recorded: no thread per child, with a test asserting it.\n\nLeft as it is. scheduler-barrier is the only workload in the catalog where ronin still trails, by roughly five to eight percent, and that gap is now fully accounted for line by line rather than merely observed. One option remains available and unmeasurable on this catalog: `polling` supports level-triggered registration, which would drop the explicit re-arm after each output drain. That re-arm never fires on this fixture because `touch` produces no output and the first drain reaches end-of-file immediately, so it would help real builds whose commands print and cannot be shown to help here. Recorded rather than done, on the principle this programme has been applying all along."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-output-pipe-not-socketpair
                        title "Give each child a pipe rather than a socket pair"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T20:23:15.859046379Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T20:23:41.728011095Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it exists because ronin-dispatch-residual-gap stopped one step too early. That node itemised the dispatch gap as syscalls and concluded every one was load-bearing. The itemisation was right and the conclusion was wrong, because it costed syscalls by count rather than by what they do.\n\nThe arithmetic that exposed it: at -j 1 the per-job difference against C samurai is about 37 microseconds, and the 5.4 extra syscalls per job explain nowhere near that. Measured directly, a bare syscall on this host costs 85 nanoseconds — mitigations are on, but retpolines and the rest do not make a syscall expensive in these terms — so 5.4 of them is under half a microsecond. The gap had to be somewhere else.\n\nMeasuring the supervising process alone, with children excluded, found it. Over 128 jobs ronin's parent executed 17.5 million instructions against samurai's 14.5 — about 23,400 more per job, which is real work rather than syscall entry overhead. The parent's profile pointed at kernel object allocation, `memset_orig` and `__memcg_slab_post_alloc_hook` prominent, and the difference in what the two tools allocate per job is exactly one thing: ronin created a socket pair for the child's output where samurai creates a pipe.\n\nThat was never needed. Output travels one way, so the second direction of a `UnixStream::pair` was dead from the start, and two `AF_UNIX` sockets carry far more kernel state than a pipe's single buffer. Measured in isolation, creating and closing a non-blocking socket pair costs about 4.9 microseconds against 2.3 for a pipe and the duplicate its writer needs. Switched to `std::io::pipe`, which is stable well below the toolchain in use and needs no dependency. Only the read end is made non-blocking, through `rustix::fs::fcntl_setfl`, because the drain loop reads until it would block while the child's end must stay blocking — a command writing faster than this process reads has to wait rather than fail with `EAGAIN`, which is why the flag cannot go to `pipe2`, since that sets it on both ends.\n\nMeasured over 128 jobs: parent instructions 17,511,983 to 16,796,921, down 4.2%, and the per-job socket pair is gone from the trace — 129 `socketpair` calls become 1, that one being signal-hook's wake pipe at startup. Wall clock is a smaller and noisier story, median 42.005 to 41.515 ms against samurai's 39.896, so about a percent; the change is worth taking on the instruction count and on being the right structure, not on the wall figure alone.\n\nIt also relocates the rest of the gap, which is the more useful result. With the socket pair gone the two parent profiles are dominated by the same kernel symbols in the same proportions — `sched_balance_find_dst_group`, `memset_orig`, `__memcg_slab_post_alloc_hook`, `clear_page_rep`, `kmem_cache_alloc_noprof`, `copy_process` — which is the process-spawn path, and identical work for both tools. The only ronin-specific entries left are our own: `Builder::build` at 2.77% and `Builder::finish_edge` at 1.59%. So the remaining roughly 17,000 instructions per job are dispatch bookkeeping in the build loop, not the poller, not the event registration, and not the syscalls that the previous node concluded were load-bearing. Whoever picks this up should start at `finish_edge`.\n\nThe correction worth carrying: costing a difference by syscall count assumes syscalls are the unit of cost, and here they were not — an 85-nanosecond syscall and a 5-microsecond one look identical in a `strace -c` tally. Count what the kernel allocates, not how often it is entered.\n\n372 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-supervisor-bookkeeping
                        title "Stop zeroing a buffer and SipHashing edge ids per job"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T20:44:14.515650388Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T20:44:39.614940186Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done. ronin-output-pipe-not-socketpair ended by localising the remaining dispatch gap to the supervising process's own code and naming `finish_edge` as the place to start. Profiling that code properly found three things, none of them where the pointer said, and all three plainly wrong rather than merely suboptimal.\n\nSizing first, because it justifies looking at all. Measuring the parent alone over 128 jobs and summing every non-kernel symbol: 18.2% of ronin's parent profile is our own code against 9.1% of samurai's, which on 21.2 and 18.9 milliseconds of parent CPU is 3.86 milliseconds against 1.72 — our supervisor costs 2.24 times what the C one does, about 30 microseconds a job against 13. That is a real target rather than a rounding error.\n\nThe first and largest was a sixteen-kilobyte buffer declared inside the output drain, so the compiler zeroed it on every call — once per job, two megabytes of pointless writes over a thousand-job build, and `__memset_avx2_unaligned_erms` was the largest single user-space entry in the profile at 1.06%. It is now a `Vec` owned by the supervisor, taken and put back around the borrow of the child it reads into.\n\nThe second was the live-children map, a `std::collections::HashMap` with the default hasher. The key is a four-byte edge identifier, looked up six or so times per job across spawn, readiness, drain and reap, and this codebase already interns and indexes everything else with rapidhash precisely because manifests are trusted input. SipHashing an integer we mint ourselves buys nothing; `std::hash::random::DefaultHasher::write` was 0.33% of the parent profile and is now absent.\n\nThe third was the same mistake in the directory cache added by ronin-stat-directory-contention: a `HashSet<PathBuf>` on the default hasher, hashing a whole path once per output per job. Now rapid-hashed, which needed a `RapidHashSet` alias next to the existing `RapidHashMap`. Related, `std::path::Components::next_back` — `Path::parent` re-parsing components — was 0.32% and is down to 0.09%.\n\nMeasured on the parent alone over 128 jobs, three runs each: instructions 17,222,188 median to 16,441,578, down 4.5%, and 6.1% below where the socket pair change started at 17,511,983. Against samurai's 14,769,757 the parent instruction gap narrows from 18.6% to 11.3%, so roughly two-fifths of it is closed. Both targeted symbols are gone from the profile entirely.\n\nAn honest gap in the evidence: wall clock could not be measured. An external clang and rustc build took the host to a load average of 45 during this work and inflated every timing by roughly a factor of five — the 128-job fixture read 208 ms where it reads 39 on a quiet machine. The instruction counts are load-independent and were taken three times per build, so the change rests on those; the wall figure should be re-taken on a quiet host before this is quoted as a speedup. Correctness is unaffected by load and was verified: 372 tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip.\n\nThe pattern worth carrying is that all three were found by reading a profile of the supervising process in isolation, with children excluded. Every earlier profile in this programme mixed the parent with the hundreds of shells and `touch` processes it spawns, which buried a sixteen-kilobyte memset per job under the kernel's fork path. When one process orchestrates many, profile it alone before concluding anything about it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T21:31:06.175952964Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Correction, on the wall measurement this node flagged as owed. It has now been taken on a quiet host and it does not support calling this change a speedup. Two corrections follow, both against my own earlier figures.\n\nFirst, the instruction reduction was overstated. The original measurement, taken while an external build held the host at load average 45, read 17,222,188 instructions before and 16,441,578 after, and was reported as −4.5%. Re-measured with the host quiet, five runs each at 128 jobs: before 16,412,708 / 16,461,429 / 16,523,324 / 16,634,003 / 16,669,933, median 16,523,324; after 16,111,956 / 16,142,070 / 16,193,654 / 16,287,624 / 16,295,879, median 16,193,654. That is −2.0%, not −4.5%. The after figure reproduces closely, 16.44 against 16.19 million; the before figure does not, 17.22 against 16.52. So the load inflated the baseline and roughly doubled the apparent gain. The claim that instruction counts are load-independent, which this programme has leaned on repeatedly and which I relied on to commit without wall evidence, is not true in general — under heavy load the instruction mix itself shifts, presumably through contention and retry paths — and it should be qualified wherever it appears.\n\nSecond, and more importantly, the wall clock shows nothing. Interleaved, randomised order, output tree and build log reset before every run, on a host at load average 1.73. At 128 jobs and -j 8, minimum-of-31: before 32.487 ms minimum and 33.767 median, after 32.202 and 33.655 — 0.9% on the minimum and 0.3% on the median, comfortably inside the run-to-run spread. At 1,024 jobs, minimum-of-15: before 249.992 and 254.561, after 249.410 and 257.956 — 0.2% better on the minimum and 1.3% worse on the median. There is no speedup here to report.\n\nThe reason is structural rather than a measurement problem. The supervising process's work overlaps with children that are already running; it lies on the critical path only in the gaps between one job finishing and the next starting. Removing two percent of its instructions therefore removes almost nothing from the wall, and the earlier reasoning in this node — which moved from \"our supervisor costs 2.24 times what samurai's does\" straight to treating parent instructions as the thing to minimise — skipped the step of asking how much of that cost is actually exposed. It largely is not.\n\nThe change is kept, on the narrower grounds that it is correct rather than that it is faster: zeroing sixteen kilobytes per job and SipHashing a four-byte identifier this process mints itself are both plainly wasteful, the fixes are small and carry no risk, and they do measurably reduce work even if that work was not on the critical path. What is withdrawn is the implication that the catalog got faster because of them.\n\nFor the record, where scheduler-barrier actually stands on a quiet host at 128 jobs: ronin 32.202 ms minimum and 33.655 median against C samurai's 30.996 and 32.414, so 3.9% and 3.8% behind — narrower than the five to eight percent this programme has been quoting, but still the one workload where ronin trails.\n\nConformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip; performance gate exit 0 with no regression."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-ninja-reference-unpinned
                        title "Record that the Ninja reference build is unpinned"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T21:31:18.359150972Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T21:31:43.679039542Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Recorded, not fixed, because fixing it means re-recording the project's reference numbers and that is not a change to make unilaterally.\n\nThe host rebooted and `/tmp` is tmpfs, so both reference builds the harnesses depend on were destroyed: `/tmp/ronin-samu-reference` and the pinned Ninja at `/tmp/ninja` and `/tmp/ninja-build`. Rebuilding them exposed the defect. The harness is strict about Ninja's *source* — `examples/conformance.rs` runs `git rev-parse HEAD` and refuses any revision other than b51a1e37c2fb89bbefa600bd155e1ce13983f09d — and silent about how it is *built*. benchmarks/README.md documents the exact compiler invocation for the C samurai reference, `cc -O2 -std=c99 ...`, and for Ninja documents only where the binary should be found.\n\nRebuilt with `cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, Ninja is roughly two and a half times faster than the recorded baseline assumes, and the performance gate failed: clean-tree-noop's Ronin/Ninja ratio \"regressed\" to 0.66x from a recorded 0.37x, with Ronin unchanged. Rebuilt with plain `cmake` and no build type — which Ninja's CMakeLists does not default for, so no optimisation flags at all — it reproduces the record closely: clean-tree-noop 29.913 ms against a recorded 29.729, manifest-command-evaluation 41.809 against the 42.8 seen in gate output through this programme, large-manifest-parse 1020.393 against 1154.622. Binary sizes tell the same story, 430,528 bytes optimised against 1,464,216 unoptimised. So the recorded baseline was made against an unoptimised Ninja, and every Ronin-versus-Ninja ratio this project has quoted is flattering by roughly that factor. Against a Release Ninja the honest figures are clean-tree-noop 7.473 ms against 11.705 rather than against 30, and large-manifest-parse 187.378 against 333.578 rather than against 1020.\n\nThree things limit the damage. The C samurai comparison is unaffected: its build is pinned by the documented `-O2` recipe and it reproduces across both runs, 10.030 and 11.059 ms on clean-tree-noop against a recorded 11.408. Every conclusion this programme has drawn about Ronin against samurai therefore stands untouched, and samurai has been the arbiter for all of it. Ronin's own absolute numbers are stable across the reboot and independent of any reference. And Ninja is not used as a correctness oracle by this defect — the conformance suite runs Ninja's own test binary, which passes regardless of optimisation.\n\nLeft in place for now: the gate has been restored to the unoptimised Ninja so it matches the record and exits 0, which is what allowed the outstanding verification on ronin-supervisor-bookkeeping to be completed. That is deliberately preserving a flattering comparison, and it should not stand.\n\nWhat a fix looks like, for whoever takes it. Pin the Ninja build the way the samurai build is pinned — put the cmake invocation in benchmarks/README.md and, better, have the harness record the reference binary's build configuration alongside the revision it already records, so a mismatch is visible in the CSV rather than silent. Then re-record baseline-v1.csv against a Release Ninja and accept that every historical ratio in the record shifts. Two smaller adjacent problems are worth folding in: `examples/baseline.rs` defaults the samurai reference to /tmp/ronin-samu-reference and drops the samurai-c comparison silently when the file is absent, so a wiped /tmp degrades the gate without any complaint; and both references living in tmpfs means this recurs on every reboot."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-intern-single-probe
                        title "Intern a new path with one probe rather than two"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T21:54:44.785221608Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T21:55:09.12128762Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done. `NodeIndex::probe` had been the largest symbol in the profile of the largest workload since the scanner work displaced `scanstring`, and nothing had attacked it since the fingerprint change. Rather than guess at it — this programme has been wrong three times today by reasoning ahead of measurement — the function was instrumented with counters and the manifest parsed to see what it actually does.\n\nParsing 100,000 statements, which interns 200,001 distinct paths: 500,003 probe calls, 1,204,229 slot examinations, 100,020 slots where the stored hash matched, 100,002 where the bytes then matched.\n\nTwo things fell out. The first is a confirmation worth recording, because it settles that the fingerprint has no headroom left: eighteen false positives across 1,204,229 steps. The expensive part of a probe — following a node identifier into `nodes` for a span and then into `paths` for the bytes, two dependent random reads — happens 100,020 times rather than 1.2 million. There is nothing further to win by filtering better.\n\nThe second is the actual defect. Five hundred thousand probes for two hundred thousand paths, and the arithmetic says where the excess is: `mknode` asked `get` whether the path was present, and on the answer \"no\" called `insert`, which walked the very same run a second time to find the very same vacancy. Worse, `insert` took only a node identifier, so before it could probe it had to read the path back out of the arena — a random read into `nodes` and another into `paths` — and re-hash those bytes. Two fifths of all probes, each over a run averaging 2.41 slots, plus 200,000 redundant hashes of forty-byte paths and 400,000 redundant random reads, all to rediscover something the first probe had already found.\n\n`get` is now a thin wrapper over `locate`, which returns both the node it found and a `Vacancy` carrying the slot index and the hash. `mknode` hands that vacancy to `fill`, which writes straight into it. The one case where the vacancy cannot be trusted is a table that grew between the two, and `fill` handles it by re-probing after `grow` — about eighteen insertions in a hundred-thousand-statement manifest pay the old cost so every other one does not. A `debug_assert` holds that a located vacancy is still vacant, which is the invariant the whole change rests on.\n\nMeasured at 100,000 statements: cycles 691,856,377 to 641,622,646 (−7.3%), instructions 1,080,296,799 to 1,046,249,875 (−3.2%), cache misses 2,120,696 to 2,047,212 (−3.5%). Wall, interleaved minimum-of-21, 162.518 to 155.507 ms and median 171.372 to 165.219, against C samurai's 294.319 and 310.546 — so 0.53x. On the suite, large-manifest-parse 173.630 ms against samurai's 322.834, and every workload in the catalog now at or ahead of samurai in that run, scheduler-barrier included at 36.101 against 36.854.\n\nWhat this does not do is make `probe` cheaper. Its share of the profile is essentially unchanged at 10.45%, because the probes removed were the cheap ones — the second walk of a pair, over slots the first had just pulled into cache. What remains is one genuinely cold read into a four-megabyte slot array per interned path, and shortening the call count cannot touch that. Anyone continuing here should target the miss rather than the count: the paths of one build statement are known together, since `parseedge` collects outputs and inputs into vectors before interning any of them, so their slots could be hashed and prefetched as a batch before the first probe. The `build all: phony ...` line, which names every output in the manifest, is the extreme case and the one most likely to show whether that pays.\n\n372 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0 with no regression."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T22:08:04.306114453Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Correction to the closing advice above, which said to target the miss rather than the count and pointed at batched prefetching. That was written from a false reading of this host's cache and should not be followed without the evidence below.\n\nThe false reading first. `lscpu` reports \"L3 cache: 512 MiB (32 instances)\" on this machine, which is a guest artifact — it multiplies the presented 16 MB by the vCPU count. sysfs gives the truth: L1d 64K, L2 512K, L3 16,384K shared across cpus 0-31, and the underlying part is a Threadripper 2950X whose real L3 is 8 MB per CCX. I had concluded from the inflated figure that the whole working set was cache-resident and never reached DRAM, which is doubly wrong: the number was fictional, and the same run had already measured 2,047,212 last-level misses, which is not \"never reaches DRAM\".\n\nMeasured properly, across three sizes spanning the L3 boundary, with working sets of 6.6, 26.6 and 53.2 MB — slot array plus node arena plus path arena — at 25,000, 100,000 and 200,000 statements. Cycles 158,073,067, 661,330,758 and 1,384,072,719, which is 6,323, 6,613 and 6,920 per statement: 9.4% growth across an eightfold size range. Last-level misses 546,293, 2,156,029 and 4,357,313, which is 21.9, 21.6 and 21.8 per statement — flat.\n\nFlat misses per statement across a working set that goes from comfortably inside L3 to three times outside it is the signature of compulsory misses rather than capacity misses. The cost is touching each new node, each new path and each manifest byte once, and that is proportional to the manifest regardless of how much cache the machine has. Capacity pressure would show as a climb past the boundary; there is none. Consistent with this, probe accounts for 6.97% of the run's misses against 10.31% of its cycles, so it is not where the misses are either.\n\nThe practical consequence is that prefetching the slot array, and the SwissTable-style SIMD group probe that would usually accompany it, are both aimed at a problem this workload does not appear to have. Probe runs average 2.23 slots, eight slots fit in a cache line, and a slot carries its identifier and hash adjacently — so a run is usually one line, and splitting control bytes into a separate array to enable a sixteen-wide compare would add a line per hit to save instructions in a loop that is already short.\n\nOne limit on all of this, stated plainly because it is the obvious objection and a fair one: everything here was measured on a single host, and one presenting a flat 16 MB L3 rather than the real per-CCX 8 MB. A machine with a genuinely smaller or differently shaped last level could behave differently. What makes me willing to rest on it anyway is that the flatness already spans both regimes — 6.6 MB at 25,000 statements sits inside even an 8 MB cache, and 53.2 MB at 200,000 sits far outside one — so the character of the access pattern is visible from both sides of that line. Anyone who does have contrary evidence from smaller hardware should reopen this; the measurement to repeat is misses per statement across sizes, which takes minutes and settles it."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-node-validation-side-table
                        title "Charge validation lists only to the nodes that use them"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T22:28:32.825215899Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T22:28:56.602484917Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it follows directly from the scaling measurement in ronin-intern-single-probe: last-level misses run at a flat 21.7 per statement across an eightfold size range, which is the signature of compulsory misses. Compulsory misses are proportional to bytes touched, so the way to reduce them is to touch fewer bytes, and the largest structure a big manifest builds is the node arena — 14.4 MB of a 26.6 MB working set at 100,000 statements.\n\n`Node` was 72 bytes: an eight-byte path span, a twelve-byte optional shell-quoted span, a four-byte optional generating edge, and two twenty-four-byte inline lists — `uses` and `validation_uses`. A third of every node was `validation_uses`, holding the edges that name it in a Ninja `|@` validation clause. Validations are a 1.11-era feature that almost nothing uses; no node in the 100,000-statement fixture has one, and the field was costing 4.8 MB of arena there to store nothing at all.\n\nIt now lives in a rapid-hashed side map on the graph, keyed by node, reached through `node_validation_uses` — which returns an empty slice for the nodes that have none — and `add_validation_use`. The feature is unchanged: six call sites across parse, build, the graph tool and compdb moved from field access to accessor, and the validation conformance tests pass untouched. `Node` is 48 bytes, down a third, and the node arena falls from 14.4 to 9.6 MB at 100,000 statements.\n\nMeasured, on a quiet host. At 100,000 statements: cycles 693,714,726 to 674,798,024 (−2.7%), cache misses 2,085,913 to 1,942,504 (−6.9%), instructions −0.6%, wall minimum-of-21 162.014 to 154.156 ms. At 200,000: cycles 1,369,836,966 to 1,323,037,612 (−3.4%), cache misses 4,064,116 to 3,863,720 (−4.9%), wall minimum-of-11 335.893 to 324.476 ms. The gain growing with size — 2.7% at 100,000 against 3.4% at 200,000 — is what a footprint change should do and is the best evidence that the mechanism is the one claimed. On the suite, large-manifest-parse 163.652 ms against C samurai's 314.060.\n\nTwo things deliberately not done, with reasons, so nobody re-derives them.\n\n`shellpath` is `Option<PathSpan>` at twelve bytes, where `PathSpan` is two plain `u32`s with no niche. Storing the path's own span instead of `None` would make it eight and remove a branch from `node_shellpath`. On its own it saves nothing: with `uses` still twenty-four bytes and eight-aligned, the struct pads straight back to 48. Worth doing only alongside a smaller `uses`.\n\n`uses` is the other twenty-four-byte list and most nodes hold zero or one entry, so a compressed layout — one flat edge vector plus an eight-byte span per node — would take `Node` to roughly 28 bytes. It is rejected for now because `uses` is not write-once: `nodeuse` appends during parse, and dyndep adds inputs to edges at build time, after any post-parse compaction would have run. Making that safe needs either a fallback for late additions or a rebuild step, and neither is worth it before the cheaper wins above are exhausted.\n\n372 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip, performance gate exit 0 with no regression."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-uses-compaction
                        title "Reject compacting the node use lists"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T22:53:39.808920801Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T22:54:02.281034586Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Rejected after reading the mutation sites. ronin-node-validation-side-table left `uses` as the last fat field in `Node` — twenty-four bytes of forty-eight, mostly holding zero or one entry — and named a compressed layout as the way to take the struct to thirty-two. That plan does not survive contact with how `uses` is actually written.\n\nThe sketch assumed `uses` is appended during parse and thereafter effectively read-only, so a flat edge vector plus an eight-byte span per node would work, with the rare late addition going to a small overflow map. Two things falsify it.\n\nFirst, most entries do not arrive during parse at all. `nodeuse` is reached from `edgeadddeps`, and `edgeadddeps` is called from `deps.rs` when the dependency log is read, from `build.rs` when a depfile is read, and from `dyndep.rs`. The first two are the ordinary path of any C or C++ build: a source with two hundred headers contributes roughly two hundred `nodeuse` calls at build time against about two at parse time. The overflow map would therefore hold the majority of the adjacency, not its tail, and the \"compacted\" representation would be a small prefix in front of a hash map — worse than what it replaced.\n\nSecond, `uses` supports removal. `parse.rs` retains against an edge when a later build statement redefines an output and the earlier edge's self-input has to be undone. A compressed row layout does not support removal without either tombstones or a rebuild.\n\nSo the structure is a genuinely mutable adjacency list with random-access insertion at two separate phases and occasional deletion, which is what `SmallVec` is already a reasonable answer to.\n\nWhat remains possible, recorded so the option is not lost. `IdVec` is `SmallVec<[T; 4]>`, whose capacity field is a `usize`, which makes it eight-aligned and twenty-four bytes. A hand-written inline vector storing a `u32` length and three inline identifiers, spilling to an index into a side arena rather than to a pointer, would be sixteen bytes and four-aligned. `Node` would fall from forty-eight to forty; and only then would packing `PathSpan` into a `NonZeroU64` pay, because the struct would no longer be padded to an eight-byte boundary — taking it to thirty-six, a further twenty-five percent off the node arena.\n\nThat is not being done now, and the reason is proportion rather than difficulty. The measured return on the previous thirty-three percent reduction of `Node` was 2.7% at 100,000 statements and 3.4% at 200,000, so a further twenty-five percent is worth perhaps two. Against that it means writing a custom container with its own spill arena, and putting it on a path that is mutated during both parse and build and that the dependency-log and depfile readers hammer — replacing a well-tested `SmallVec` on exactly the code where a subtle bug would corrupt the build graph rather than merely slow it. The trade is poor at this point and should only be revisited if the node arena is again shown to be the binding constraint.\n\nA correction to my own earlier note while here: ronin-node-validation-side-table says the `uses` compaction is blocked because \"dyndep adds inputs to edges at build time\". That understates it — dyndep is the rare case; the dependency log and depfiles are the common one, and they are what makes the overflow design unworkable."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-real-project-validation
                        title "Validate against real cmake-generated projects"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T23:06:33.426419063Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-01T23:06:58.837038825Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Every measurement this programme has made was against a fixture this programme wrote. Brendan asked whether a real repository was needed to know how any of it goes, which was the right question, and the answer turned out to be yes for one reason and no for another.\n\nTwo real manifests were generated with CMake's Ninja generator and built to completion with ronin so their dependency logs held real header sets: the Ninja project itself, 224 build statements and a 120,211-byte manifest, and abseil-cpp, 917 statements and a 484,429-byte manifest. Then ronin, C samurai and a Release-built Ninja were compared on the three things a build tool actually does between compiles, interleaved in randomised order, minimum of fifteen, on a quiet host.\n\nNinja project, 224 statements: no-op rebuild 4.121 ms against samurai's 4.728 and Ninja's 5.235; `-t commands all` 1.761 against 2.211 and 3.039; a dry run after touching a widely included header 4.578 against 4.844 and 6.112. abseil, 917 statements: no-op 8.907 against 10.785 and 10.232; commands 3.817 against 6.652 and 6.077; touched-header dry run 7.508 against 11.552 and 9.729. Ronin leads all three tools on all six measurements.\n\nThe reason the question was worth asking is that the margin is scale-dependent and the small project hides it. Against samurai, the command-evaluation ratio goes 0.80x at 224 statements, 0.57x at 917, and 0.52x on the synthetic hundred-thousand-statement fixture; the touched-header dry run goes 0.95x to 0.65x. At 224 statements a run is four milliseconds and ronin's own startup floor is about one of them, so a quarter of the measurement is process creation and the real difference is compressed. Anyone judging this work on a small project would conclude the margin is a few percent. It is not — but they would be right about their project.\n\nThe reason the answer is also no is that the synthetic fixtures turn out not to be flattering. The trend across 224, 917 and 100,000 statements is monotonic and smooth, so the large fixture is the natural endpoint of a curve visible in real code rather than an artifact of how it was generated. That is a stronger justification for the large-manifest workload than it had before, which was an argument about Chromium-shaped paths.\n\nOne specific worry was refuted. The suite's dependency-log fixture uses 300 edges with a single header each, and I had flagged it as unrepresentative of real dependency loading and suspected it might be hiding a weakness — particularly since ronin's node use-lists are appended to once per recorded dependency. Real fan-in is two orders of magnitude larger: the Ninja project records a median of 276 headers per edge across 20,383 entries, abseil a median of 300 across 44,687. On those, ronin's no-op rebuild — which loads the whole dependency log and stats every node — is 0.83x samurai and 0.87x Ninja. The fixture was hiding a strength, not a weakness, and the `SmallVec` use-lists that ronin-uses-compaction declined to replace hold up under real fan-in.\n\nLimits worth stating. 917 statements is still small; abseil is largely header-only, so only 159 of its edges carry dependencies at all. Nothing here reaches the ten-thousand-statement range where a Chromium or LLVM tree sits, and the only evidence at that scale remains synthetic. The two projects are also both CMake output, so nothing here says anything about GN, Meson or hand-written manifests, which differ in rule count, variable depth and how much work `subninja` does. Reproducing this needs CMake and a network fetch, so it is not suitable for the gate; it is a periodic check rather than a regression test.\n\nNo code changed. The artifacts live at /tmp/ninja-real and /tmp/absl-build and will not survive a reboot, which is the same tmpfs problem ronin-ninja-reference-unpinned records for the reference binaries."
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T11:27:33.1385048Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Findings from measuring 25,000 to 200,000-edge manifests on 2026-07-31, and one methodology correction that matters more than any of them. Callgrind has been the primary instrument for this whole programme and it is a user-space simulator that does not count kernel instructions. On a 200,000-edge fixture it reported ronin 3,074,971,466 against samurai 2,729,279,505, a ratio of 1.13, while perf reported 5,331,534,241 against 2,939,783,556, a ratio of 1.81 — the entire difference being kernel instructions ronin executes and samurai does not. Any conclusion this programme drew from callgrind about syscall-adjacent work is unsound, and the batched-stat node already recorded that callgrind and getrusage both mislead on threaded work. Use perf for anything touching syscalls, callgrind only for pure user-space comparisons, and interleaved wall clock as the arbiter. Getting perf working needed kernel.perf_event_paranoid lowered from Debian's default of 3 to 1, which also wants kernel.kptr_restrict at 0 for kernel symbols. A retraction belongs here too: an earlier claim that ronin runs 1.7x slower than C samurai at every realistic project size was wrong, and wrong because of a fixture defect of my own making. That fixture declared 200,000 sources that did not exist on disk, so both tools error out — but samurai discovers the missing input during its plan walk and bails after 3 stat calls while ronin stats 400,001 first, so the comparison measured one tool short-circuiting against the other doing full work. Re-measured on parse-and-evaluate through the commands tool, which both tools complete, the real curve is 0.83x at 4,000 edges, 0.89x at 25,000, 0.94x at 50,000, 0.99x at 100,000 and 1.06x at 200,000 — a genuine but gentle erosion from seventeen percent faster to six percent slower across a fifty-fold size range, not a constant 1.7x. Disk-side caching of the parsed graph was considered and dropped without a node: profiling the large run puts the genuinely cacheable work — scanstring, canonpath, append_variable and parse — at about 27%, while mknode, wide_hash, node_for and the allocator, which together are more, would simply become deserialization. A load-free mapped arena format could do better, but at that point a resident daemon dominates it, which is what Bazel and Buck2 do."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-29T22:58:22.604396347Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @note
                text "Follow-up programme for the eleven findings from the full blind Rust review. Preserve Ninja compatibility, the byte-exact and dense-arena decisions, and the pinned conformance oracle. Existing compatibility rules remain owned by the shipped nodes; each leaf starts as target-impl scaffolding and must gain or revise internal specification rules before implementation when it introduces a normative safety, error, or performance guarantee."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-mimalloc-tuning
        title "Measure mimalloc and single-unit release tuning"
        priority 1
        deps (ronin-release-tuning ronin-iterative-tools)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-30T07:44:56.02532914Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-30T08:07:07.74090331Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "mimalloc measured slower than glibc malloc and is rejected. Root cause is workload shape, not the allocator: ronin is a short-lived (15-40 ms), single-threaded coordinator, so mimalloc's segment reservation and first-touch page faults are never amortized, and its contention advantages (sharded heaps, lock-free cross-thread free) never engage. The jemalloc A/B in benchmarks/span-frontend-2026-07-30.md shows ~193k allocation requests for a 4,000-edge workload (~48 per edge) — the problem is allocation count, not allocator speed. Keep glibc malloc; retain the measured codegen-units=1 single-unit release tuning. Allocator choice is revisited only after the ronin-allocation-discipline programme drops allocations-per-edge to single digits (see ronin-allocator-revisit)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-30T08:12:55.838147547Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

