    {
        id ronin-output-styles
        title "Make build output rendering selectable"
        children (
            {
                id ronin-reporter-seam
                title "Route build status and failures through a reporter"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-01T23:34:45.361975176Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-01T23:40:29.402926708Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Done. Every byte a build writes about itself now goes through one trait rather than being assembled inline in the supervisor.\n\nBefore this, `emit_status` built the status line by hand — pick description or command, expand the template into a `String`, splice the description into it if the format came from `--status`, append a newline — and `command_finished` separately assembled the `FAILED:` block from a `format!` and a loop over the edge's outputs. Two places, both writing Ninja-shaped bytes, neither nameable from outside. A second rendering could not be added without either a second branch at each site or a copy of both.\n\nThe seam is `Reporter`, with `status` and `failure`, both appending into a caller-owned `Vec<u8>` and returning nothing. That shape is deliberate in three ways. Renderers cannot fail, so error handling stays where the writing is and the supervisor keeps its existing `emit`/`flush_sinks` path untouched — including the branch that buffers into `build_output` when there is no sink, which the library API depends on. Renderers do not own the sink, so nothing about streaming, flushing or console-pool ordering moves. And rendering appends rather than returning, so the buffer can be reused.\n\nIt is reused: `emit_rendered` moves the scratch buffer out of the builder, renders, writes, and puts it back. Moving rather than borrowing is what lets a renderer hold `&self.progress`, `&self.options` and `&*self.graph` while the bytes accumulate. The status path previously allocated a `String` for the template and then grew a fresh `Vec` per command as the description and newline were appended; with `--status` it allocated a third buffer to splice the description into. Now one warm buffer absorbs all of that and the template `String` is the only allocation left. `format_progress_status` returning `String` is what keeps it there, and an `_into` variant would remove it, but that function carries the ported `formatstatus` spec annotations and is not worth disturbing for this.\n\nNo performance claim is being made and none was measured. The change adds one indirect call per finished command to a path that already performs a `write(2)` and a flush per command, which is not a ratio worth instrumenting, and the buffer reuse it introduces is a reduction in allocator traffic rather than a claimed speedup. The measurement worth taking is on the renderer that comes next, not on this.\n\nWhat the change has to prove instead is that nothing moved, and that is what was checked. `NinjaReporter` reproduces the previous bytes exactly, including the description-or-command rule, the `0x1f` marker expansion for CLI-supplied formats, and the `FAILED: [code=N] ` block with its trailing space after each output path. 376 tests pass, four of them new and pinning the rendering directly — counter and description, a command with no description standing for itself, `--verbose` preferring the command, and a CLI format placing the description at every marker. Clippy clean, fmt clean, and the conformance suite green at 425 upstream Ninja tests, 10 differential CLI cases and the persistence round trip. The upstream suite is the real evidence here: it parses Ronin's build output, so a single byte out of place in the status line or the failure block fails it.\n\nOne thing this deliberately does not do is choose a rendering. There is one implementation and no way to select another, which is unused generality until the Cargo renderer lands on top of it. That is the intended order — the seam is the reviewable part and the renderer is the visible one, and separating them means the commit that changes no output can be verified against the oracle on its own."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-01T23:41:56.712692557Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-cargo-output-style
                title "Render build status in Cargo's verb-column style"
                deps (ronin-reporter-seam)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-01T23:42:01.665731539Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-01T23:55:30.881085239Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Done. Ronin can now render a build the way Cargo does — a right-aligned verb, then what was acted on — selected by `--output cargo`, with `--color auto|always|never` deciding escapes.\n\nThe verb comes from the description's first word. That sounds like a guess and is instead the observation that generators already write descriptions in exactly that shape: CMake and Meson emit `Building CXX object …`, `Linking CXX executable …`, `Generating …`, so `Building CXX object CMakeFiles/demo.dir/a.cc.o` splits into a verb and a subject with no manifest changes, no new bindings, and nothing for a generator to opt into. Two cases fall outside it and are handled rather than approximated. A description of one word becomes all verb and no subject. A command shown in place of a description — no description at all, or `-v` — gets the verb `Running` and the whole command as subject, because splitting the first word off `/usr/bin/c++ -c a.cc` would put a path in the verb column, which is where an action belongs.\n\nColour is a separate decision from rendering, and both are explicit. `[dec:ronin:product-boundary]` is what settles this: Ninja owns the compatibility vocabulary, so Ninja's rendering stays the default and nothing about a terminal changes which rendering is used. A tool that parses Ronin's output sees identical bytes on a pipe and on a tty. Colour is the one thing terminal detection does decide, and only within `--color auto`: escapes are emitted when stdout is a terminal, suppressed when `NO_COLOR` is set to a non-empty value, and forced either way by `always` or `never` — `always` deliberately overrides `NO_COLOR`, on the convention that the variable overrides a default rather than an instruction. That is recorded as a new requirement, `product.output-style`, alongside the existing product-boundary rules.\n\nWhere terminal detection happens is a design point rather than an implementation detail. A build writes through a `dyn Write` that may be a terminal, a pipe, or a buffer a library caller owns, and none of those can be asked which it is. So the question is asked once at the process boundary, of the real descriptor, and carried in as a `TerminalContext` on the `Runner` — which is where `[spec:ronin:req:runtime.explicit-invocation-boundary]` already says process-global values get snapshotted. `Runner::new` leaves it false, so an embedded caller gets no escapes unless it asks for them.\n\nBrendan caught the dispatch choice, and he was right. The first cut stored the renderer as `Box<dyn Reporter>`, which was reflex rather than reasoning — I had written the word \"trait\" when proposing the seam and then implemented the word. Static dispatch is better here, but not by the obvious route: a type parameter on `Builder` would monomorphise the entire build loop, dirty evaluation and dependency ingestion once per rendering, to devirtualise a call that happens once per finished command directly next to a `write` and a flush. An enum gets the same direct, inlinable call for a branch on a value fixed before the first command starts, with no vtable, no allocation, and no duplicated caller. `Reporter` is now `enum { Ninja, Cargo(Palette) }`.\n\nThe palette is resolved escapes rather than a flag, so a colourless build appends three empty slices instead of testing three branches, and both paths read the same.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent: progress counters. Cargo shows progress in its bar, not on each line, and the bar is ronin-progress-bar, which is the node after this one. Until then `--output cargo` trades `[3/7]` for the verb column, and `--output ninja` — the default — still has counters for anyone who wants them today. The `Finished N commands in T` summary partly covers the loss, and prints only when at least one command ran and the build did not fail.\n\nTwo limits worth stating. Verb alignment is by character count where the description is UTF-8 and by byte count where it is not; neither is a true display width, so a wide or combining character in a verb sits a column out. That is alignment, not correctness, and every generator's verbs are short ASCII words. And `ronin: no work to do.` and `ronin: subcommand failed:` are still emitted in their existing form under `--output cargo`; they are Ronin-owned CLI diagnostics rather than build output, and restyling them was not in scope.\n\nMeasured only as far as it should be at this stage: nothing. This adds a branch per command on a path that already syscalls per command, and the honest measurement is of the bar, which is the part with an actual redraw budget. 390 tests pass — 15 new, covering the verb split, a verb at or past the column, a one-word description, a command standing in for a description, colour not shifting the column, the summary and its absence for Ninja and for an empty build, `NO_COLOR` resolution across all three choices, option parsing in both `--opt value` and `--opt=value` forms, rejection of unknown values by name, and two end-to-end builds asserting a whole run's bytes in Cargo style, including the failure block. Clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip — which is the evidence that the default rendering did not move, since the upstream suite parses Ronin's build output."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:03:35.127114628Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Amendment: the Cargo rendering carries a progress counter after all, dimmed and trailing.\n\nThe original shipped without one on the reasoning that Cargo shows progress in its bar rather than on each line, and that ronin-progress-bar would restore it. Brendan rejected that: on a real project the counter is one of the only ways to know how far in a build is, and deferring it to a node that has not been built yet leaves the rendering worse than the default it is offered as an alternative to. He is right, and the reasoning was backwards — Cargo can omit per-line counts because it always draws a bar, so copying the omission without the bar copies the wrong half.\n\nPlacement was the real question, and it was put to him with three renderings to compare. He chose the counter at the end of the line, `    Building CXX object CMakeFiles/libninja.dir/src/build.cc.o (12/83)`, dimmed so it recedes.\n\nThe case for the leading counter, which was the recommendation and was not taken, is that a trailing count jitters horizontally because descriptions vary in length, so it cannot be scanned down the way Ninja's can. The case against it, which is what the choice preserves, is that anything in front of the verb column is as wide as the numbers currently in it, so the column would shift as a long build passed nine, ninety-nine and nine hundred and ninety-nine finished commands — and the verb column is the entire point of the rendering. Trading a jittering counter for a stable verb column is the right way round for a style whose value is the left edge.\n\nThe count is `finished/total`, the same pair `%f/%t` gives in Ninja's default, and taken at the same moment: after the increment for the command being reported. Console-pool commands report at start rather than finish, where Ninja's counter has the same shape, so the two renderings agree about what the numbers mean. The failure block carries no counter — the status line for that command already printed one immediately above it.\n\nDimming is `\\x1b[2m`, added to the palette as a fourth resolved escape, so a colourless build still appends an empty slice and the code path does not branch.\n\n390 tests pass with the pinned strings updated to include the counter, plus the end-to-end build asserting `(1/1)` in Cargo style and still asserting the absence of Ninja's `[1/1]` form. Clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:11:34.765587963Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Measured: the Cargo rendering costs nothing against the Ninja one, and getting that answer required throwing away the first two attempts at measuring it.\n\nThe workload isolates rendering. A dry run after touching a widely included header plans every dependent edge and emits one status line per edge without spawning a single child, so the status path — render, write, flush — runs at full rate with no compiler noise. Output goes to `/dev/null`, so the write syscall per line is still paid and no terminal is involved. Two real CMake-generated projects: abseil-cpp at 917 statements and 252 status lines, and the Ninja project at 224 statements and 70.\n\nInstructions, fifteen interleaved randomised repetitions, minimum and median. abseil: Ninja 40,684,674 / 40,696,410; Cargo 40,630,065 / 40,654,863; Cargo with colour 40,659,440 / 40,673,460. Ninja project: 16,223,110 / 16,229,581; 16,217,277 / 16,227,363; 16,227,185 / 16,234,862. The Cargo rendering is 0.13% *fewer* instructions than Ninja's on abseil and 0.04% fewer on the Ninja project, and colour costs 0.07% on top of that. Within-mode spread between minimum and median is about 0.03%, so the abseil difference is real and the Ninja-project one is not resolvable.\n\nThe direction is the right way round for the wrong reason to be pleased about it. Ninja's rendering allocates a `String` and walks the status template for every line; the Cargo rendering pads a column, appends three or four slices, and formats one counter. Those two are within about 165 instructions per line of each other, which at 252 lines is 42,000 instructions against a run of 40.7 million. Nothing here justifies choosing one rendering for speed, which is the useful result: the choice is entirely a matter of what someone wants to read.\n\nWall clock says the same thing less precisely. Forty-one interleaved randomised repetitions, minimum of each: abseil 9.443, 8.917 and 9.503 ms; Ninja project 4.977, 4.983 and 5.061. The Cargo rendering is fastest on abseil by minimum and slowest by median, which is what a difference below the noise floor looks like.\n\nNow the part worth recording properly, because both earlier attempts produced confident wrong answers.\n\nThe first attempt reported the Cargo rendering 4.6% more expensive, and the second 7.9%, both with a ±0.3% run-to-run spread that made them look solid. They were an artifact of the measurement. Ronin's dry run appends to `.ninja_log` — one entry per planned edge, 20,090 bytes per run on abseil — so every run loads a larger log than the one before it. The perf batches ran Ninja mode first and Cargo mode second, so Cargo was always loading a log that fifteen Ninja runs had just grown. Interleaving fixed the ordering bias; restoring a recompacted log before every single run fixed the drift. Absolute counts fell from 185 million to 40.7 million once the log was restored, and abseil's dry-run wall time fell from 17.6 ms to 9.4 ms — my own repeated measurement runs had grown that log to 6.7 MB, so I had spent the earlier attempt measuring the cost of loading my own test residue.\n\nTwo lessons, neither new to this plan and both apparently still needed. Interleaving is not optional even for an instrument as steady as an instruction count, because the bias it removes is ordering, not noise, and a low variance says nothing about a monotonic drift. And a workload that mutates the state it reads is not a workload until the state is restored between runs — the `scheduler-barrier` fixture already gets reset between runs for exactly this reason, and I did not think to ask whether a dry run was equally stateful. It is, and that it is at all is a separate finding, recorded on ronin-dryrun-log-divergence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:18:19.511218405Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-progress-bar
                title "Pin a throttled progress bar to the terminal"
                deps (ronin-cargo-output-style)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:18:24.987937513Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:30:04.895684665Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Done, and it costs what the design said it would: seven extra write syscalls on a run that makes four thousand.\n\nThe bar is a line held at the bottom of the terminal — `    Building [=========>              ] 24/83: Building CXX object src/graph.cc.o` — with the build scrolling above it. It appears with the Cargo rendering when the output is being styled, which means `--color=always` forces it out exactly as it forces escapes out. Tying the bar to colour rather than to terminal detection directly is deliberate: both answer the same question, \"is this being written for someone to look at\", and one knob is better than two that would almost always agree. It also makes the thing measurable without a pseudo-terminal, which is how the numbers below were taken.\n\nThe cost control is the repaint budget, and it is the whole design. Clearing the bar is free — the erase sequence is appended to the same buffer as the line that displaces it, so it rides out in a write that was happening anyway. Painting is a separate write, and a build of trivial commands finishes them far faster than a terminal can usefully show. Refusing to paint more than thirty times a second bounds the bar at thirty writes per second regardless of how fast the build runs, rather than one per command. Because a skipped paint leaves no bar drawn, the next line's clear is a no-op too, so the erase traffic is bounded by the same budget.\n\nMeasured, interleaved and randomised, `.ninja_log` restored before every run because ronin's dry run appends to it. A 2048-edge dry run — 4096 start and finish events in about 28 ms, a far higher event rate than any real build reaches — makes 4097 writes to stdout in Ninja's rendering, 4098 in Cargo's, and 4105 with the bar. Seven writes. It paints twice in the whole run. Instructions over 21 repetitions: Ninja 65,988,960, Cargo 65,648,470, Cargo with bar 66,607,381 — the bar is 1.46% above the same rendering without it, or about 234 instructions per event, which is the clock read the budget check needs plus the memcpy that records what to name. On abseil-cpp's 252-line dry run, fifteen repetitions: 40,745,460, 40,689,583 and 40,836,510 — the bar is 0.36% above Cargo without it, and paints exactly once for the whole run. Wall clock resolves none of this and was not expected to.\n\nSo the honest summary is that the bar is free on real projects and costs about one and a half percent of a synthetic worst case, and that the per-event charge is the budget check itself rather than any drawing. If that ever mattered, the fix is to check the clock once per supervisor wake rather than once per event, not to draw less.\n\nThree things the implementation had to get right beyond drawing.\n\nAnything written while a bar is drawn has to displace it first, and that includes bytes the renderings never see — a command's own stdout. So the erase goes through one place that every emitting path calls, and a compiler diagnostic takes the bar's line before printing rather than landing on top of it.\n\nThe bar must be given back whatever the outcome. `finish` now runs on the failure and interruption paths too, not only on success, so a failed build does not leave a painted bar for the shell prompt to land on; the summary line is still success-only. The build's own error outranks any trouble writing that closing line, so the outcome is chosen before the closing write is attempted.\n\nAnd the flush count is a tested invariant, not an implementation detail. The first cut flushed unconditionally after painting and broke `ronin_build_flushes_each_completed_output_batch_once`, which pins one flush per completed output batch under `runtime.process-supervisor-scalability`. Painting now reports whether it wrote anything, and only a paint that wrote causes a flush — so a rendering with no bar, which is every Ninja build, has exactly the syscall pattern it had before.\n\nTwo limits, stated rather than discovered later. Terminal width is read afresh on each paint rather than captured at startup, because it is the one process fact here that changes while a build runs and a stale width means a wrapped bar, which the single-line erase cannot take back; the read is capped by the same thirty-per-second budget. It reads stdout's width specifically, so an embedded caller that sets `is_terminal` while writing somewhere other than stdout would get the wrong width — the bar would still be correct, just sized for a different terminal. And the bar names the most recently started command rather than listing everything in flight, which is what Cargo does; a list would need per-command bookkeeping and an allocation each, and the subject buffer is reused precisely to avoid that.\n\n397 tests pass, seven new: no bar when the output is not being styled, the gauge filling at nought, half and full, the erase emitted once and not twice, a repaint refused inside the budget and allowed once it refills, the line given back on both success and failure, truncation that does not split a character in UTF-8 or run past the end in bytes, and an end-to-end build asserting nothing is left on screen after the summary. Clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-02T00:48:05.212771004Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Wall clock confirms the bar is not slower than Ninja's rendering, which was the question.\n\nSynthetic worst case, 2048-edge dry run with 4096 events, 101 interleaved randomised repetitions on a quiet host (load 0.51 at start, 0.55 at end), `.ninja_log` reset before every run: minimum 14.45 ms for Ninja's rendering, 14.51 for Cargo's, 14.44 with the bar. Medians 15.72, 15.82 and 16.08. The minima are indistinguishable and the median spread is a third of a millisecond on a workload whose event rate no real build approaches.\n\nOn a real build the difference is not resolvable at all, and cannot be: six interleaved full clean rebuilds of the Ninja project per rendering came out 13,640 ms minimum for Ninja's and 13,248 for the bar's, which would make the bar faster — it does not, there is no mechanism, and the within-rendering spread of 783 ms is three times the difference. That run was also taken with the host under load average 13 to 18 and should not be quoted. The bound it establishes is that any real difference is under six percent of a 13.6-second build, against a predicted effect of about 0.15 ms, so it neither confirms nor refutes anything. The high-event-rate figures above are the ones that answer the question."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-dryrun-log-divergence
        title "Stop the dry run appending to the build log"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-02T00:11:57.602191042Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found while measuring the Cargo rendering, not while looking for it. Ronin's dry run writes build-log entries for commands it did not run; Ninja's does not.\n\nEvidence, on abseil-cpp with a freshly recompacted 20,225-byte `.ninja_log` restored before each tool. `ninja -n` leaves it at 20,225 bytes. `ronin -n` leaves it at 40,315 — an append of 20,090 bytes, one entry per planned edge. C samurai also opens the log for writing on `-n`: its run recompacted a bloated 6.79 MB log down to 20,225, which it could not have done without write access. So this is inherited from the C implementation rather than introduced in the port, and it is a divergence from the compatibility oracle, which is Ninja and not samurai.\n\nWhat it is not: a wrong build. The obvious failure mode would be a dry run recording the new command hash for an edge, so that a later real build compares the manifest against a log that already agrees and skips the work. That was tested directly — build an output, change the rule's command, dry run, then build for real — and the rebuild happens, matching the control that omits the dry run. Nothing observed so far produces a stale artifact.\n\nWhat it is: unbounded log growth and a measurement hazard. Every dry run adds an entry per planned edge with no upper bound short of recompaction, so a workflow that dry-runs often — an editor integration, a pre-commit check, a benchmark harness — inflates the log indefinitely, and every subsequent invocation of any tool pays to load it. That is not hypothetical: repeated dry runs during this session grew abseil's log from 20 KB to 6.7 MB, which doubled the dry-run wall time from 9.4 ms to 17.6 ms and silently invalidated two rounds of measurement before the cause was found. Anything benchmarking ronin with `-n` is measuring its own residue unless it restores the log.\n\nOpen questions for whoever takes this. Whether the upstream suite has a case for it at all, since 425 pinned tests pass with the divergence present — if there is no such test, the differential harness is the place to add one. Whether the entries are written for every planned edge or only some. Whether `.ninja_deps` has the same problem: it did not change size across these runs, but a dry run plans no dependency ingestion, so absence of evidence is weak here. And whether the fix is to suppress log writes under `dryrun` at the recording site or to decline to open the log for writing at all, the second being the stronger guarantee and the one that matches what `ninja -n` observably does."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T00:48:27.969301808Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T00:50:37.464936139Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Fixed. The dry run no longer writes to `.ninja_log`, and now matches Ninja byte for byte on a recompacted log: abseil's stays at 20,225 bytes through `ronin -n` where it previously grew to 40,315.\n\nThe write was one unguarded call in `finish_edge`. The deps-log writes immediately above it were already inside `if !self.options.dryrun`, and the build-log write simply was not — so this is the guard the surrounding code already establishes, applied to the one site that missed it. The in-memory mtime and dirty-state updates are deliberately left outside the guard, because the rest of the dry run's planning depends on them: an edge the run pretends to have built has to make its dependents plan correctly.\n\nWhat this does not change is worth stating, because it bounds how much the bug mattered. A dry run never produced a wrong build. The failure mode to worry about was a dry run recording the new command hash for an edge, so that a later real build compared the manifest against a log that already agreed and skipped the work — that was tested directly before the fix, by building an output, changing the rule's command, dry running, then building for real, and the rebuild happened. What the bug actually cost was unbounded log growth: one entry per planned edge, every time, so any workflow that dry-runs often inflated the log without limit and every subsequent invocation of any tool paid to load it. That is not hypothetical — repeated dry runs during this session took abseil's log from 20 KB to 6.7 MB, which doubled its dry-run time and invalidated two rounds of measurement before the cause was found.\n\nC samurai has the same behaviour, so the port inherited it rather than introducing it. The oracle is Ninja, so it is still a divergence.\n\nThe open question from the original report is answered and the answer is unwelcome: all 425 upstream tests passed both before and after, so the pinned suite does not cover this. The differential harness is the right place for a case, and it is not added here — this commit is the fix, and the harness gap is a separate piece of work. A regression test is in place at the unit level: a dry run leaves no log entry and produces no output file, and a real build immediately after still runs the command and does record it.\n\n398 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-retire-c-corpus
        title "Retire the C samurai corpus from the repository"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-02T00:57:11.953556275Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T00:58:58.196952927Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. The 4,637 lines of C that Ronin was ported from are out of the repository — twenty-six sources and headers, the Makefile, and the `samu.1` manual page.\n\nThe interesting part is what it did to spec coverage, which improved rather than degraded. The nspec configuration carried two impls: `source-impl`, tracking the original C symbols across `**/*.c **/*.h` in `@impl-only` mode, and `target-impl` for the Rust in `@both` mode. Deleting the C would have orphaned 331 `[spec:ronin:def:…]` and `[spec:ronin:sem:…]` annotations, so `source-impl` went with it. Coverage moved from 201 of 205 rules covered with 2 stale to 201 of 201 covered with none stale, and the two long-standing unplanned symbols, `samu.main-fn` and `samu.parseenvargs-fn`, are gone. Those four rules described the C entry point and its environment parsing, which Ronin deliberately does not implement — `product.no-samuflags` is the requirement that says so. They were only ever covered because the C that satisfied them was sitting in the tree.\n\nSo the requirement text is still the specification and the Rust still has to implement and verify every rule; what has been dropped is a second implementation of the same spec that nobody was going to change again.\n\nTwo things had to move with it. The performance harness built its C samurai reference from these sources, so `benchmarks/README.md` now clones upstream samurai instead; `examples/baseline.rs` needed no change, since it only looks for the binary at `/tmp/ronin-samu-reference` and already omits the `samurai-c` rows when it is absent — which is a silent degradation the harness has been warned about before and still has. And `src/port_tests.rs` stays: despite the name it is pure Rust behaviour tests carrying the `/test` annotations for a large share of the spec, with no build-time dependency on the C at all.\n\nThe LICENSE keeps every notice it had. Ronin is a translation of samurai rather than an independent implementation, and htab and tree descend further back through Myrddin and musl, so the ISC, Apache-2.0 and both MIT grants still govern the Rust sources whether or not the C is distributed beside them. A copyright line for the Rust work was added above them, and a sentence saying explicitly why the rest is retained, so that the next person to look does not read the removal of the C as licence to prune the notices.\n\n398 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip — the last of which matters most here, because it proves compatibility is still being demonstrated against the pinned Ninja oracle rather than against the C corpus that just left."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-spec-namespace
        title "Rename the spec namespace from samurai to ronin"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-02T11:15:44.663345192Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T11:18:20.431706742Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. The spec namespace is `ronin`, so rules read `[spec:ronin:req:compat.scheduling]` rather than `[spec:samurai:req:…]`. 1,063 annotations across 65 files, plus the nspec spec name and the plan name.\n\nMechanical, but with one judgement call in it. The rewrite matched the exact token `spec:samurai:` rather than the word `samurai`, because the two are different things now: the namespace was the last place the port's origin leaked into the product's own vocabulary, while every prose mention of samurai in the benchmark records refers to the actual C tool that Ronin is still compared against. Those are untouched and must stay — `benchmarks/README.md`, the alloc-metrics and baseline records, and the two performance-validation CSVs all name samurai as a measured competitor, not as an ancestor.\n\nNode logs were rewritten along with everything else. A historical note referring to `[spec:samurai:req:x]` refers to the rule that is now `[spec:ronin:req:x]` — same rule, renamed — so leaving the old spelling would have left dangling references in the record rather than preserving anything true.\n\nOne non-obvious site: `scripts/check-release.sh` greps `docs/spec` for stale requirement annotations with a literal pattern, which the rewrite caught because it contained the same token. Had it been spelled differently the release gate would have silently stopped finding anything and passed for the wrong reason.\n\nVerified rather than assumed: coverage is unchanged at 201 of 201 rules covered, 182 tested, none stale; `nplan_audit` reports no orphan references, and shipping is back `on_ref` now that the plan's ref follows the branch rename to `main`. 398 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-eta-prediction
        title "Record edge durations and predict progress the way Ninja does"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-02T13:47:41.16237236Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T13:57:34.883043282Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Done, and the advertised compatibility level is 1.14.0. Brendan asked whether we could just bump the version token; the answer was no, and finding out why turned up two defects rather than one.\n\nThe question came out of an audit. Every feature the pinned 1.14 manual marks as post-1.9 was checked against Ninja directly: dyndep, `-t cleandead` and `-t restat` from 1.10; validations, `-t inputs` and `-t missingdeps` from 1.11; the `%E %w %W %P` status codes from 1.12; `-t multi-inputs` from 1.13; the `$^` newline escape and `-t restat --builddir` from 1.14. All implemented, all matching — except the 1.12 status codes, which were an edge-count approximation of something Ninja does quite differently.\n\nNinja weights progress by how long each edge took *last time*. `RecalculateProgressPrediction` maintains the split between edges the build log has timed and edges it has not, and reports `%P` as `cpu_time_millis_ / total_cpu_time_millis` rather than finished-over-total. `%E` and `%W` then scale elapsed time by that fraction. Ronin counted edges. The two agree only when there is no history to weight with, which is why the first comparison looked clean — the harness was deleting `.ninja_log` between runs, so both tools were in their degenerate case. With history present, on one 0.8-second edge followed by three instant ones, Ninja reported 99% and an ETA of 6 ms where Ronin reported 25% and 2.4 seconds. Ronin was wrong in the direction people notice: it claims a quarter done when the expensive quarter is already behind it.\n\nThe second defect is why the first could not simply be fixed in the formatter. Ronin was passing literal zeroes for the start and end fields of every `.ninja_log` entry, so there was no history to weight with, ever. Ninja writes millisecond offsets from the start of the build — `0 305`, `305 608` — and reads them back through `ParsePreviousElapsedTimes`. Ronin wrote `0 0` for everything. That is a cross-tool defect and not just an internal one: Ninja reading a log Ronin wrote lost its own prediction and silently fell back to counting edges, which the persistence round-trip test never caught because it checks structure rather than the time fields.\n\nBoth are fixed. Edges now carry their launch offset from preparation through to completion, and the recorded entries match Ninja's shape. The prediction is a translation of Ninja's algorithm including the parts that look like superstition and are not: it will not trust the previous build's timings until fifteen seconds and five percent of edges have passed, and then only if this build's average edge is within a factor of ten of last build's — the case that guards against is a ccache hit last time and a miss this time, where a stale prediction is worse than no prediction.\n\nOne ordering bug on the way, worth recording because the symptom was so specific. The first working version reported `0% 99% 99% 99%` against Ninja's `99% 99% 99% 100%` — every value shifted one edge late. Retirement was happening in `finish_edge` after `command_finished` had already rendered the status line, so each line described the state before its own edge landed. Ninja accounts for the edge and then prints. Moving the accounting to the top of `finish_edge` fixed it, and it has to be there rather than later for a second reason: the previous duration must be read before this run's entry overwrites last run's in the log.\n\nVerified against Ninja in all three regimes: with history `%P` is 99/99/99/100 for both and `%E` agrees to within timing jitter; without history both give 25/50/75/100 and matching ETAs; in a dry run both give 0% and `?`, which is the case Ronin previously answered with 100% and 0.000. Interop is bidirectional — Ninja reading a Ronin-written log now predicts 99%, and Ronin reading a Ninja-written log does too.\n\nWith that, the bump is honest. `NINJA_COMPAT_VERSION` is 1.14.0 and the `ninja_required_version` gate derives from the same constant, which is what let the two drift apart in the first place: the parser refused everything past 1.9 while the implementation had grown through 1.14. The consequence was sharper than a stale number. Ronin already implemented `$^` *and* its rule that using it requires declaring 1.14 — so the feature was unreachable, because declaring 1.14 was itself refused. A test now asserts the reported token and the gate agree.\n\nNot changed, and recorded rather than fixed: Ronin's too-new-version diagnostic is samurai's wording, `ninja_required_version 1.15 is newer than 1.14`, where Ninja says `ninja version (1.14.0.git) incompatible with build file ninja_required_version version (1.15)`. That divergence predates this work, is specified in docs/spec/port/parse.md, and is not covered by the differential harness. It should be settled deliberately rather than folded into a version bump.\n\nAlso recorded: `tests/performance_baseline.rs` pinned Ronin's rows in the recorded validation CSV by the literal `ronin,1.9.0,` prefix. The recorded measurements are still the ones the gate compares against, so the assertion now matches on tool and workload and leaves the recorded data untouched rather than rewriting history to suit a new constant.\n\n399 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-version-diagnostics
        title "Match Ninja's wording for the version-check diagnostics"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-02T17:09:47.062827392Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-02T17:10:08.184039176Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. The `ninja_required_version` diagnostics are Ninja's, word for word, and the check has the branch it was missing.\n\nThe wording was samurai's: `ninja_required_version 1.15 is newer than 1.14`, where Ninja says `ninja version (1.14.0.git) incompatible with build file ninja_required_version version (1.15).` A manifest asking for too new a Ninja is something generators and CI logs read about, so inheriting the port's phrasing was a compatibility gap rather than a stylistic one. Both now match apart from the product name and the version token, which are Ronin-owned under `[dec:ronin:product-boundary]` — `ronin: fatal: ninja version (1.14.0) incompatible …` against `ninja: fatal: ninja version (1.14.0.git) incompatible …`.\n\nReading Ninja's `CheckNinjaVersion` to get the wording right turned up a branch Ronin did not implement at all. Ninja has three outcomes, not two: fatal when the manifest wants a newer version, silence when the versions are compatible, and a *warning* when the supported major is greater than the requested one — `ninja executable version (…) greater than build file ninja_required_version (0.9); versions may be incompatible.` A manifest written for Ninja 0.x builds, and says so. Ronin accepted those in silence.\n\nImplementing that needed something Ronin did not have: any way to emit a warning. There is no warning channel in the codebase — `-w dupbuild=warn` suppresses an error rather than raising a warning, and `-w phonycycle=warn` is parsed and then ignored entirely. So this adds one, shaped by where the constraint actually is. The parser cannot write warnings itself, because a manifest may be read through a library caller's sink or through no sink at all; where a diagnostic goes is the invocation's decision. So the parser collects them and the CLI drains them, streaming to the diagnostic sink when the invocation has one — so a warning appears while the build runs, as Ninja's does — and buffering into the result's stderr when it does not.\n\nThat channel is the reusable part of this change. The two accepted-and-ignored warning flags now have somewhere to report to, and neither is wired up here; they are separate behaviour with their own upstream semantics and should be done deliberately rather than smuggled in behind a version-check fix.\n\nVerified against Ninja on all three branches: `0.9` warns and builds in both, `1.5` is silent in both, `1.15` and `2.0` are fatal in both with matching text. Two tests pin it end to end through the public `Runner`, one asserting the fatal message and one asserting the warning reaches stderr with a zero exit code. The requirement text in docs/spec/port/parse.md now describes all three branches rather than the two the port had.\n\n399 tests plus 9 CLI tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-warning-flags
        title "Make the -w warning flags do what Ninja does"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:38:47.360166743Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:39:11.08669177Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. Both `-w` flags now behave as Ninja's do, where before one was silently wrong and the other did nothing at all.\n\n`dupbuild` was the wrong one. Ronin carried samurai's behaviour: `dupbuild=warn` suppressed the duplicate-output error and built anyway. Ninja deprecated the flag — both spellings now emit `deprecated warning 'dupbuild'` and change nothing, because duplicate outputs have been unconditionally fatal since. So the `dupbuildwarn` option is gone rather than renamed: there is no mode in which a duplicate is tolerated, and keeping a switch for it would preserve a divergence. The parser test that asserted the manifest was accepted now asserts it is rejected, which is a behaviour change worth stating plainly — a manifest that built under `-w dupbuild=warn` before this will fail after it, and would have failed under Ninja all along.\n\n`phonycycle` was the one that did nothing: the flag was parsed, matched, and explicitly skipped. Ronin already filtered self-referencing phony statements — the CMake 2.8.12 and 3.0 shape — but silently, and `=err` had no effect at all. Now the filter runs only under `=warn`, which is the default and reports `phony target 'a' names itself as an input; ignoring [-w phonycycle=warn]` byte-for-byte with Ninja, and `=err` leaves the self-edge in place so cycle detection sees it and the build fails as it should.\n\nNinja's shape restriction came across with it, deliberately: the diagnostic applies only to a phony edge with exactly one output, no implicit outputs and no implicit inputs, because that is the form old CMake emitted. A phony that mentions itself in any other shape is a genuine cycle and is not filtered.\n\nOrdering matters and cost a fix. Command-line warnings are emitted before the manifest is read, as Ninja's are, rather than being seeded into the parser's list — the first attempt did the latter and the deprecation warning vanished whenever the manifest then failed to parse, which is exactly the case where `-w dupbuild=warn` is most likely to be passed.\n\nOne gap left open and recorded rather than half-closed. Under `=err` Ronin now fails, but says `dependency cycle` where Ninja says `dependency cycle: a -> a [-w phonycycle=err]`. Reporting the path requires an active-node stack threaded through `DirtyEvaluator::evaluate`, which is the iterative dirty-evaluation loop this programme has optimised repeatedly; adding a push and pop per edge to it for a diagnostic is not a change to make casually or without measuring. It belongs with the broader manifest-message parity work, where the same question is asked about every other diagnostic at once.\n\nVerified against Ninja: `=warn` matches exactly, `=err` fails in both where it previously passed in Ronin, and the deprecation warning matches. Four new CLI tests cover both flags in both modes through the public `Runner`. 399 tests plus 11 CLI tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-manifest-diagnostic-shape
        title "Render manifest diagnostics in Ninja's located shape"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:48:05.315557083Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:48:26.362520915Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Manifest diagnostics now have Ninja's shape: a located header, the offending source line, and a caret beneath it.\n\nBefore, a scan error read `build.ninja:2:24: invalid $ escape` and a parse problem read `undefined rule 'nosuchrule'` with no location at all — even though the span was right there in the error, unused, because `ManifestError`'s `Display` matched `Self::Problem { problem, .. }` and threw the span away. Both now read as Ninja does:\n\n    error: build.ninja:3: undefined rule 'nosuchrule'\n    build o: nosuchrule\n             ^ near here\n\nThe rules came from `Lexer::Error` rather than from taste, and several are non-obvious enough to be worth writing down: the header carries the line but not the column; the context block is omitted entirely when the column is zero or at least seventy-two; a line longer than seventy-two characters is cut there and marked with an ellipsis; and the caret sits under a column counted from zero, where Ronin counts from one.\n\nOne diagnostic is deliberately exempt. A too-new `ninja_required_version` goes through Ninja's `Fatal` rather than through the lexer, so it carries no file, line or caret — it reads `fatal: ninja version (1.14.0) incompatible with build file …` and nothing else. The first cut routed it through the located renderer and produced `error: build.ninja:2: fatal: …`, which is two severities and a location Ninja does not print; the CLI test written for that message caught it.\n\nTwo parity gaps remain open, and both are larger than they look.\n\nThe anchor is wrong. Ninja positions a diagnostic at `last_token_`, the start of the most recently read token; Ronin positions it at the scanner's current index. For `build o: nosuchrule` that puts our caret past the end of the token instead of under its first character, and for a rule variable it reports the line after the one at fault, because the trailing newline has already been consumed and the line counter has moved on. That inaccuracy is not new — the spans were always computed at that point — but it was invisible until the location started being printed, and printing a wrong line number is worse than printing none. Fixing it means the scanner recording the start of each token, which is three stores per token across every scan entry point, on code this programme has optimised twice. Cheap in principle, invasive in practice, and it wants measuring rather than assuming.\n\nThe wording is also not Ninja's. `undefined rule` against `unknown build rule`; `unexpected rule variable` against `unexpected variable`; `build has no outputs` against `expected path`; `multiple rules generate 'o'` against the same without quotes. That is roughly twenty `ManifestProblem` variants plus the `ScanError` kinds, each needing its Ninja counterpart found and verified rather than guessed. It is a bounded piece of work and a tedious one, and it belongs with the anchor because the same test would cover both.\n\nFour of Ronin's own tests pinned the old un-located text and were updated; where the located header now embeds a temporary directory, they assert the message tail rather than the whole string. 399 tests plus 11 CLI tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-log-content-check
        title "Compare build-log content, not just its signature"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:57:51.080570854Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T11:58:11.051264472Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The conformance harness now compares what goes into `.ninja_log`, not just the signature on its first line. Two real defects had already walked past the old check.\n\n`verify_state_signatures` read the file, confirmed it began `# ninja log v7`, and stopped. Under that, Ronin recorded every command with zeroed start and end times — costing Ninja its progress prediction whenever it read one of our logs — and a dry run appended a full set of entries for commands it had not run. Both files had perfectly good signatures.\n\nThe new check builds the same fixture from clean with each tool and compares the records: same outputs, same command hashes, in the same order. Timing values cannot match between two runs and are not compared to each other; what is checked is that they are coherent — no command ending before it began, and not every duration zero, which is precisely the shape the old bug had. The mtime column is dropped entirely, since it is the output's real modification time and differs between runs for reasons that say nothing about compatibility.\n\nThen it asserts a dry run changes nothing.\n\nThat last part is the piece worth recording, because the first version of it passed with the bug deliberately put back. The check ran its dry run on the tree the clean build had just left up to date, so nothing was planned, nothing was written, and every tool passed trivially — including a Ronin patched to log on dry runs. The check was asserting that a no-op is a no-op. It now deletes two outputs first so there is real work to plan, and refuses to draw any conclusion if the tool plans nothing, on the grounds that a check which cannot fail is worse than no check because it reads as coverage.\n\nBoth branches were then verified by reintroducing each defect and watching the harness reject it: `Ronin recorded no command durations at all` for the zeroed timings, and `changed the build log during a dry run` for the other. The dry-run defect is also caught earlier and more cheaply by the unit test added when it was fixed — which is why the first attempt at verifying it here proved nothing, since `cargo test` failed before the harness ran. Checking the harness in isolation, against a binary built with the bug, is what exposed that the harness itself was not doing its job.\n\nConformance now reports five lines rather than four: 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, the persistence round trip, and build-log records with dry-run inertness."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-no-work-predicate
        title "Judge an all-phony plan up to date as Ninja does"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T13:40:32.746834534Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T13:40:53.638350049Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Fixed: a plan holding only phony work is up to date, so `ronin: no work to do.` is printed where Ninja prints it.\n\nFound while setting up the three-way comparison, not while looking for it. On abseil-cpp a no-op build printed nothing at all — Ninja said `ninja: no work to do.`, C samurai said its equivalent, Ronin said nothing and exited zero. On the Ninja project's own build directory Ronin printed the message correctly, which is what made it look like a project-specific oddity rather than a semantic difference.\n\nIt is a semantic difference. `already_up_to_date` tested `self.plan.is_empty()`, which is the wanted-edge count; Ninja's `Plan::more_to_do` is `wanted_edges_ > 0 && command_edges_ > 0`. The two agree except on a plan that wants edges but has no commands behind them, which is exactly what abseil's `default all` produces — a phony over phonies, with every real output already up to date. Ronin concluded there was work, ran none, and suppressed the message on the grounds that it was not up to date. The Ninja project's graph never leaves a wanted phony behind in that state, so it never showed there.\n\nWorth noting what this was not: Ronin planned zero commands in both cases and did no extra work, so the three-way comparison taken alongside it stays valid. The divergence was confined to the message.\n\nThe fix uses `command_edge_count`, which already existed for the progress total, so the predicate now reads the same two quantities Ninja reads. A regression test builds a manifest that is nothing but phony edges and asserts the plan is up to date.\n\nThis is the second compatibility defect this session that a real project surfaced and the fixture-based suite did not, after the dry-run build-log writes. Both are the same shape: a graph feature the hand-written fixtures happen not to contain. The differential harness fixture has one phony edge (`build all: phony result`) but that phony sits over a real command, so it never reaches the state that matters. Widening it to include a phony-over-phony default would cost nothing and is worth doing when the manifest-message parity work touches that harness anyway.\n\n400 tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-command-launcher
        title "Separate command interpretation from process spawning"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T14:37:28.701326341Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T14:45:01.383843135Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. Interpreting a command and spawning a process are now separate decisions, and a command the shell would only split into words is executed without one.\n\nThe measurement that made this worth doing: a trivial job costs 1.741 ms through `/bin/sh -c` against 0.852 ms spawned directly — the shell is 51% of it. Brendan's point is what reframed that. I had sized the idea against C builds, where a job is a ~100 ms compile and the shell is half a percent, and concluded it was not worth the risk. That is the wrong denominator for a large class of real users: linguistics and NLP pipelines drive Make and Ninja with tens of thousands of stamps and small transforms, where the per-job overhead *is* the build. In that regime this is not a micro-optimisation, it is halving the work. It also means scheduler-barrier — 128 `touch` edges, which I had been dismissing as an artificial fixture that only isolates dispatch — is a faithful model of that workload rather than a curiosity. I was wrong to keep discounting it.\n\nWhat is emphatically not being done is implementing a shell. The `command` binding's meaning is the shell's, and on Unix that is not \"POSIX sh\" in the abstract but whatever `/bin/sh` resolves to on the machine — dash here, bash-in-POSIX-mode elsewhere, busybox ash on Alpine. So the detector is deliberately over-broad: any of `` |&;<>()$`\\\"'*?[]~#{}! `` or a tab, newline or carriage return anywhere in the command sends it to the shell untouched. `#` only opens a comment at the start of a word and `~` only expands there, but a command containing either falls back rather than inviting an argument about where exactly. Being wrong in this function does not crash, it runs a different command than the manifest asked for, so it errs toward the shell every time.\n\nOne case needed singling out. A leading `VAR=value` is an assignment to the shell and the name of a program to `execvp`, and `=` is not otherwise significant — so `FOO=1 cmd` would have slipped through a naive scan and tried to exec a program called `FOO=1`. It is rejected explicitly, with a test.\n\nThe hazard that mattered showed up exactly where predicted, and the fix is better than what was planned. A missing program produced `ronin: No such file or directory (os error 2)` on the direct path against Ninja's `/bin/sh: 1: …: not found`, because the spawn now fails in the parent rather than in a shell. The plan had been to emulate that text — which would have pinned Ronin to dash's wording and broken on any system whose `/bin/sh` is bash. Instead a direct spawn that fails with `NotFound` or `PermissionDenied` is retried through the shell, which then produces its own diagnostic and its own exit status. Correct by construction rather than by imitation, and the wasted spawn attempt costs nothing on a path that ends the build anyway.\n\nMeasured coverage on real manifests: 68 of 83 commands on the Ninja project need no shell, 159 of 253 on abseil. The blocker in both is `&` — CMake writes `cd X && …` for custom commands and `: && cmake -E rm -f … && ar qc …` for archives. Compile commands, which are the bulk, are metacharacter-free.\n\nThe surface is `--compat`, which spawns a shell for every command exactly as Ninja does, and `--shell`, which selects a different one — with `--shell none` meaning the automatic behaviour explicitly. `ShellMode` has a `Program` variant ready for the dash port Brendan is writing, which will be able to interpret commands in-process on Windows as well, where Ninja has no shell at all and passes the raw string to `CreateProcess`. That is a deliberate divergence and is now written down as `product.command-execution` rather than left implicit.\n\nRecorded, not fixed, because it is not this change's doing: Ronin exits 1 when a command fails where Ninja exits with the command's own status — `exit 3` yields 3 from Ninja and 1 from Ronin. C samurai also exits 1, so this is inherited from the port. It matters for CI, where the exit code carries information, and it deserves its own node.\n\n405 tests pass, five of them new — the detector across plain commands, every significant character, leading assignments, the empty command, and space collapsing — plus two through the public `Runner` asserting that a shell-free command produces identical output with and without `--compat`, and that a missing program still gets the shell's diagnostic. Clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. Spec coverage 202 of 202.\n\nNot yet measured: the wall-clock effect. The host has been busy all afternoon and a queued measurement already gave up once; quoting a number taken against contention would be worse than quoting none."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-failure-surface-parity
        title "Match Ninja's build-failure and directory-change surface"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T22:49:13.631639708Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T22:52:23.202534709Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. What Ronin does when a build fails, and what `-C` announces, now match Ninja byte for byte across 22 measured scenarios.\n\nThis began as two divergences found in five minutes while the host was too loaded to measure anything. Enumerating the surface rather than fixing them one at a time turned up twelve, and then more: the final count is five distinct defects plus one deliberate reversal.\n\nThe enumeration is the part worth keeping. Rather than fix what was in front of me, I wrote a script that runs Ninja and Ronin over a scenario list and diffs exit status and each stream separately, without stopping at the first mismatch. Twelve of fourteen scenarios differed. Every one of them was in a shape the existing harness already checks — `compare_case` compares rc, stdout and stderr byte for byte — but the corpus was ten `-t` tool invocations over a fixture with no failing rule, never run with `-C`. The harness was sound and under-asked. That is the same finding as the last two sessions: the fixtures do not contain the graph or the failure the real world does. This time the fix is corpus rather than a single case.\n\nThe defects:\n\n**Exit status was thrown away.** `exit 7` gave 7 from Ninja and 1 from Ronin. C samurai also exits 1, so it was inherited. Ninja's contract, read out of `ParseExitStatus` and then measured: a child that exited reports its own code; a child killed by SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP reports 130; anything else reports the *raw wait status* plus 128 — not the signal plus 128. That distinction is visible: a dumping SIGQUIT reports `FAILED: [code=259]`, which the OS then truncates to 3 on exit. It is Ninja's arithmetic and it is in the output, so it is reproduced rather than corrected, with the reason written down where someone will otherwise fix it.\n\n**An interrupted command was reported as a failed one.** Ninja tests for the interrupt before it finishes the command, so a build cut short by SIGTERM prints no `FAILED:` line at all. Ronin printed one and then said `subcommand failed`. Ronin also treated SIGQUIT as an interrupt, which Ninja does not — Ronin handles SIGQUIT arriving at itself, but in a child it is an ordinary failure.\n\n**`build stopped:` went to the wrong stream with the wrong words.** Ninja prints `ninja: build stopped: REASON.` on stdout after the build's own output; Ronin printed `ronin: subcommand failed: <the command>` on stderr. The reason is not read off the last error but off how much failure allowance was left: exhausting it says the build was cut off (`subcommand failed`, plural above `-k 1`), having some left says everything remaining depended on something already broken (`cannot make progress due to previous errors`).\n\n**`-C` announced nothing.** Zero occurrences of \"Entering directory\" in the whole tree. That line is not decoration: Emacs compile-mode and every error parser that inherited GNU Make's convention read it to resolve the relative paths a compiler prints, which is why Ninja's comment calls out the funny quotes. Without it, jump-to-error lands in the wrong file. It goes to stdout, before the move is attempted so a directory that cannot be entered is still named, and is suppressed for tool modes and `--quiet`. The failure message was wrong too — `No such file or directory (os error 2)` against Ninja's `fatal: chdir to 'x' - No such file or directory`; Rust appends an error number to the text Ninja quotes from `strerror`, so the suffix it added is removed again.\n\n**`-C` composed when Ninja's does not.** Ninja keeps one `working_dir` that each `-C` overwrites, so `-C a -C b` resolves `b` against the invocation directory and fails if it is not there. Ronin resolved it against `a` and succeeded. C samurai chdirs sequentially and Ronin followed it; there was a test asserting the samurai behaviour, which is now inverted. The move is also deferred until the whole command line is read, as Ninja defers it, which is what lets the announcement know whether a tool was selected.\n\n**A failing manifest regeneration is not a build outcome.** Ninja reports `error: rebuilding 'build.ninja': subcommand failed` on stderr and exits 1, dropping the command's status. This one is worth recording carefully: the scenario for it *passed* before the fix existed, because a generator edge with no dirty input is already up to date and never ran. It was asserting that nothing is nothing. Giving it a dirty input exposed the divergence immediately. That is the second time in three sessions a check has passed vacuously; the pattern to distrust is a fixture that has to reach a state, rather than one that starts in it.\n\n**The reversal, which deserves an argument.** An interrupted Ninja exits 130. C samurai re-raises the signal so it dies by SIGINT, and Ronin followed samurai — deliberately, recorded on ronin-signal-handling as \"shell-compatible re-raise semantics\". Making the build path match Ninja left Ronin inconsistent with itself: an interrupt during the build exited 130, an interrupt before it still re-raised. Re-raising is the better convention — it is how a shell learns the user interrupted a loop rather than that one iteration failed — but the contract is Ninja's, and the inconsistency was not defensible either way. Both paths now exit 130. This is written down as a rule rather than left in a commit message, because it reverses a decision someone made on purpose.\n\nTwo departures from Ninja are deliberate and stated in `product.build-outcome`. A plan that can make no progress without having recorded a failure returns success from Ninja — it reads its accumulated exit code, which is still zero — while printing `stuck [this is a bug]`. A build that did not finish must not report that it did, so Ronin leaves with a failure. And Ninja's raw-wait-status arithmetic is reproduced rather than corrected, since it is observable.\n\nOne thing found and left alone: Ninja returns immediately when it cannot *finish* a command (a bad depfile, a stat failure), where Ronin counts it as a keep-going failure and carries on. I could not produce a case where the two differ — the obvious one, a missing depfile under `-k 0`, behaves identically in both — so I did not restructure the scheduler on a hypothesis. It is written here so the next person looking has the map.\n\nThe corpus is now 18 cases in the harness, covering exit status through the process, the four signal dispositions, keep-going with and without allowance left, console-pool failure, missing input, manifest regeneration, and six `-C` behaviours. It normalises exactly two things: the temporary path, and the product name at line start with its colon — so a stray \"ninja\" inside a path or a message stays a real difference.\n\n408 unit tests, 15 integration tests, clippy clean, fmt clean. Conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 18 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. Spec coverage 203 of 203.\n\nNot measured: nothing here should move wall time — it is diagnostics and exit paths — but the launcher measurement from the previous node is still outstanding, and the host has been at load 40 all evening from an external clang/rustc build."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-cycle-path
        title "Name a dependency cycle by the path around it"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T22:59:50.032134012Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:00:08.443608515Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. A cycle is now reported as `dependency cycle: a -> b -> c -> a` rather than `dependency cycle`, which said only that one exists.\n\nThe known case was `-w phonycycle=err`, where Ninja appends the flag name so the reader knows which setting turned a warning into an error. Checking it first showed the gap is general: no cycle of any kind reported its path, phony or not. Telling someone their graph has a cycle without saying where is close to useless on a manifest with tens of thousands of edges, which is the only size at which this happens by accident.\n\nNinja assembles the path from its DFS stack, and two details in it are easy to get wrong and were worth reading rather than guessing:\n\nThe cycle is reported from the node that *closes* it, not from whichever node happened to enter the shared edge. Ninja overwrites the stack entry (`*start = node`) with a comment giving the exact case: building `b` where `build a b: cat c` and `build c: cat a` must report `a -> c -> a`, not `b -> c -> a`. A multi-output edge can be entered through any of its outputs, and naming the one that was asked for instead of the one in the cycle points at the wrong place.\n\nThe `[-w phonycycle=err]` suffix is narrower than it looks. It appears only when the cycle is a single node *and* the edge is phony with exactly one output, no implicit outputs and no implicit inputs. Ninja's comment says why: CMake 2.8.12 and 3.0 emitted `build a: phony … a …`, and the flag exists for that shape alone. Any other cycle is an ordinary one, and mentioning a flag that would not have suppressed it would be misdirection.\n\nRonin's evaluator is an iterative worklist rather than recursion, so there was no call stack to read the path off. It now keeps a `path` vector alongside: one entry per active edge, holding the node that edge was reached through, pushed where `Work::Finish` is pushed and popped where it is handled. That is exactly the set of edges on the way down, because `Finish` is pushed before an edge's inputs and popped only after all of them are done.\n\nBoth cycle-detection sites feed it. The third construction site, in the missing-deps root scan, reports that every output is used by something and never walked a path; it keeps the pathless message, which is what Ninja prints there too.\n\nVerified against real Ninja on five shapes: the phony self-cycle with the flag, a two-node phony cycle, a three-node cycle through real rules, a cycle through a multi-output edge asked for by the other output, and the same through phony. All five match byte for byte. Four are now in the conformance corpus, which is up to 22 cases, plus a binary-level test that asserts the rendered text without needing Ninja present.\n\n408 unit tests, 16 integration tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-diagnostic-anchor
        title "Point a manifest diagnostic at the token it is about"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:05:33.927661259Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:05:59.849232553Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Done. A manifest diagnostic now names the line the reader has to go and fix.\n\nThe recorded symptom was a caret in the wrong column. Checking it showed the line number was wrong too, and that it was wrong in the way that matters most: an unexpected binding inside a rule was reported against line 4 when the offending line was 3, because by the time the parser rejected the binding the scanner had already read the `build` keyword on the next line. Being told the wrong line is worse than being told no line, since it sends the reader somewhere that looks fine.\n\nOne cause. Ninja's lexer keeps `last_token_`, the start of the token it is reading, and every diagnostic is computed from it — line, column, and whether any source context is shown at all. Ronin computed all three from the current scan position, which by the time an error is raised has usually moved past the token and often onto the next line. `Scanner` now keeps the same mark, set where each token begins, and both error constructors read it instead of the position.\n\nThat one change fixed three symptoms at once, which is the sign it was the right level to fix it at. The line number, because the mark is on the token rather than past it. The caret column, likewise. And *whether there is any context at all* — Ninja emits the source line and caret only when the column is greater than zero, so `unexpected indent`, whose token starts at column zero, has never had a caret in Ninja. Ronin printed one. The rendering code already implemented that rule correctly, including the 72-column truncation; it was being handed a position that made the rule produce the wrong answer. Ronin now also prints none, and the initial mark sits at offset zero so a diagnostic raised before any token behaves the same way.\n\nOne case needed reading Ninja rather than reasoning about it. A complaint about a rule binding lands on the character that *ended* the value, not on the value: `nonsense = x` puts the caret one past the `x`. That is not a designed choice, it falls out of `ReadEvalString` marking each chunk as it consumes it, so after a value the mark sits on the newline that stopped it. I had anchored at the value's start, which is more defensible and does not match. The scanner now marks again at the end of a value scan, before trailing whitespace is eaten, with a comment saying why so it does not read as a mistake.\n\nVerified against Ninja on the unexpected rule binding, the unexpected pool binding, an unknown build rule, a missing colon, and an unexpected indent: every location, caret column and presence-of-context now agrees. A binary-level test pins the anchoring without needing Ninja present.\n\nNot fixed here, and now the only remaining difference on every one of those cases: the message wording. Ronin says `unexpected rule variable` where Ninja says `unexpected variable`, `undefined rule` for `unknown build rule`, and `expected ':'` for `expected ':', got newline ($ also escapes ':')`. Worse, `duplicate rule` carries no location at all, because it is raised as a graph error rather than a manifest one and never reaches the located renderer. That is the next node, and it is the reason no manifest-diagnostic cases were added to the conformance corpus yet: they would compare wording that is still known to differ.\n\n408 unit tests, 17 integration tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 22 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-manifest-message-parity
        title "Say what Ninja says when a manifest is wrong"
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:18:26.397545662Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:18:52.778792193Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "28 of 33 manifest diagnostics differed from Ninja's. 5 still do, and they are named below rather than left to be rediscovered.\n\nThe node said \"~20 variants\" from an earlier reading of the error enum. Enumerating properly — a case per error string in Ninja's `manifest_parser.cc` and `lexer.cc`, compared byte for byte on both streams and the exit status — found 33 shapes and 28 differences. Counting the variants was the wrong way to size this; the difference between the two is that several variants had the wrong *shape*, not the wrong words.\n\nFour kinds of difference, in rising order of how much work they were.\n\n**Words.** Ronin said `unexpected rule variable` and `unexpected pool variable` where Ninja says `unexpected variable` for both; `undefined rule` for `unknown build rule`; `build has no outputs` for `expected path`; `invalid pool depth 'x'` where Ninja does not quote the value; `multiple rules generate 'a'` where Ninja does not quote the path; and two samurai-inherited sentences — `rule 'cc' has no command` and `rule 'cc' has rspfile and no rspfile_content or vice versa` — where Ninja says `expected 'command =' line` and `rspfile and rspfile_content need to be both specified`. Mechanical once seen.\n\n**A missing distinction.** Ronin had one `expected name` for every position; Ninja names the position — `expected pool name`, `expected rule name`, `expected variable name`, `expected build command name`. Reading `expected name` on a `build` line tells you nothing you did not know. The scanner's name reader now takes what it is reading a name for. Likewise `expected ':'` became `expected ':', got newline ($ also escapes ':')`: Ninja names the token it found, and adds the colon hint because a path containing one is the usual reason to be there.\n\n**Diagnostics with no location at all.** A duplicate rule, a duplicate pool, an unknown pool name and an unreadable `include` were raised as graph or I/O errors and never reached the located renderer, so they printed a bare sentence with no file, no line and no context. `ronin: No such file or directory (os error 2)` for a missing include is close to useless in a generated manifest tree. All four are now located manifest problems, and the include failure reads `loading 'x.ninja': No such file or directory` against the line that asked for it.\n\n**Where Ninja stands when it complains.** Several checks run only once a statement's indented block is over, so Ninja's diagnostic names the line *after* the block and shows no source context — its column is zero, and Ninja suppresses context there. Ronin reported them against the statement's own line with a caret. A rule with no command, a pool with no depth and a half-specified response file now report from the same place Ninja does. This also uncovered a detail worth having: an error with no context ends with a *blank line*, because Ninja terminates the message itself and the printer terminates the line again. Confirmed in the bytes with `od`, not inferred.\n\n**The five that still differ**, all one shape: Ninja defers the build-statement checks until after the statement's bindings, so `empty path`, `dyndep 'x' is not an input`, `multiple rules generate x` and `unknown pool name 'x'` name the following line and carry no context, while Ronin reports them where they are found. The words and the exit status match; the location is one line early and there is a caret Ninja does not print. Moving them means restructuring edge parsing so the checks run after the binding loop, which is a real change to that function rather than a rewording, and it is the whole of what is left. The fifth is a tab where an indent belongs: Ninja's lexer does not accept a tab as an indent at all, so the rule block simply ends and the complaint is `expected 'command =' line` on the next line; Ronin reports `tabs are not allowed, use spaces`, which is Ninja's wording for a tab found somewhere else. Both say a tab is wrong; they disagree about which error it is.\n\nTen of the fixed cases are now in the conformance corpus, which is at 32. The corpus is what makes this stick — the previous count of these had been done by reading the enum, and reading found roughly two thirds of them.\n\n408 unit tests, 17 integration tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 32 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks."
            }
        )
    }
)
